   253	She would send word up the road by the first carter going that way: how late she was, and why, and where to send for her if the breathing changed. It was all that could be done from here, and she had done it a dozen times in thirty rounds, and it had never once felt like enough. She had learned, a long while ago, to tell the things that could be done from the things that could only be carried, and to put the second sort where they did not get into her hands.
   254	
   255	She went over the top without turning to see Thornwater below her. There was a mule in that yard that would be standing under three boxes by now. She had read the beast before she left her, as she read anything of hers: a sound heart, and a broad slow bed, and no notion whatever that she had been given away.
   256	
   257	She had told the big one that a person should not name a beast.
   258	
   259	She had never named that one. At the wicket she had learned that this made not the least difference.
   260	
   261	---
   262	
   263	She slept that night in a carters' halting-ring on the far side of the hill, alone, and she was colder than she had been on any road in years.
   264	
   265	It was the mule again. She had lain against that warm brown flank on a good many frozen nights and had never once thought of it as a thing that she owned. She lit no fire. It was a road; and besides, she had her back against a chock-stone and a great deal to go over, and she went over it better in the dark.
   266	
   267	She began with her hands, as she always did.
   268	
   269	They were as they had been. She had mended eleven people at that gate since a morning on a plank, and every one of them had come up under her palms with banks and a bed. She had laid them on a girl's arm a fortnight old and felt eight places take. Nothing in them was missing. She had told the boy so, since he had a right to know what he had not broken.
   270	
   271	There was a piece of her craft in him, all the same. She knew that as she knew a mend of her own on somebody else's leg. She had felt it go along beside her and then go on alone.
   272	
   273	She put no question to herself about what he was. She had heard a small pale person say, by a lantern, that there was a thing she would not write down; she had seen a calfskin with rings on it, and heard a quiet man say *yet*. Those were other people's trades. She had gone into that loft with one thing that she knew, and she had said it, and come down a ladder.
   274	
   275	She thought about the line instead, since that was hers.
   276	
   277	She had crossed it eight times in the grey, where nobody was to see, and one person had seen and held his tongue for twelve days. She had gone in with her palms up, and with two fingers on her own wrist, and backward, and with her eyes shut; and she had stood in it for a count of a hundred with her arms hanging. Each time it had been the same. On one side of a grey stone she was a Tide, Iron, of the fourth rank, with a reserve like a purse. On the other she was a woman in a dead man's coat who could tie a knot.
   278	
   279	She had not minded it as much as she had expected, and that was the thing that she had gone in eight times to be sure of. A carter with a split palm did not care which side of a stone her hands had last worked on. And on the evening after that noon she had bound a girl's arm inside the line with a man's folded cuff and strips of her own shirt, having nothing else, and the binding had held until six people could get the arm out to where a Tide was a Tide.
