   282	
   283	So she would be at the edge. There would be another of those places; the small one had as good as promised it. Somebody ought to stand on the right side of the stone with her sleeves pushed up.
   284	
   285	She came to the last of it then, which she had been keeping.
   286	
   287	He had tried to pay her at the well. She had seen it coming across the yard, in the way that he carried the book; he was a boy who could not bear to owe, and he had wanted to be rid of the debt before she went, as a person wants a splinter out, without much caring how it is drawn.
   288	
   289	It had been her teacher's saying, about a single morning. She had heard it over a basin, forty times, with a ruler in the woman's other hand. A reading is a thing that the reader does, on a day, in a temper. The first tells you about the temper. It is the third that begins to tell you about the patient.
   290	
   291	He would read himself twice more. He was frightened of what he had found, and he would do it regardless, since he had been told to. Then he would sit on some bench of hers and wait his turn, and say it; and she would be the first living person to hear what the thing that she read with had made of the one body that it could not read.
   292	
   293	She wanted that. She looked squarely at the wanting, with her back against a cold stone, as she had once looked at it on a road out of Oxhollow. She had not forgiven him. She did not expect that she would, quite. But she had once been hungry for sixteen days to know how it was that he could not be read, and she was hungrier now to hear what he had found.
   294	
   295	The road bent in the morning.
   296	
   297	It had gone north from the wicket, and over the low place, and north again. At the first water it turned along the bank, as a carter's road will, and by the middle of the day she had the sun on her left cheek, and by the afternoon it was in her eyes. The water grew. Another came into it, and a third. There were alders, and then a hedge, and a place where wheels had been in and out of the stream for a great many years.
   298	
   299	A little before dusk she heard it working.
   300	
   301	It was a slow heavy knocking, a long way down the valley, with water falling under it. She knew that sound. There would be a wheel at the end of it, and a fence, and a keeper who counted people off a ridge. Beyond the fence there would be a bench, with whoever had sat down on it sixteen mornings ago when her mule should have come in sight.
   302	
   303	She shortened nothing and lengthened nothing. She shifted the rope on her shoulder, and went down toward the sound at the one pace that she had, on the low side of nothing at all.

