   186	
   187	He knew where it was. He had known for five years, like a man knowing where a step on a stair is uneven: a place in himself that Brom's Iron read had caught the edge of once on a floor in Ardenmere, with no Tide practitioner in the city, at no stakes, and had never caught again. He had been round it a thousand times since without seeing it, as he had been round every current in him for eighteen years without hearing one. He went to it now with the new thing, slowly, the way she had gone along the ninth, and found it.
   188	
   189	It was there.
   190	
   191	He lay very still with his hands on himself and catalogued it as sensation, because he had promised her the finding and had promised the log no verb. It was not a current. That was the first thing and the plainest: everything else in him *went*, and this did not. It was not the tenth; the tenth was what he was feeling it with, and a hand is not the thing it touches. It was not any of the nine; he had their sounds now, and this had none. It was a place. Near the middle — under where her front hand had been, where everything was *nearer* — and the ten flows went round it. Not through. Round, the way the currents in a river go round a thing on the bed that they have gone round for so long that the going-round is the river's shape and not the thing's.
   192	
   193	He did not name it. He did not have a name that was not a guess, and a guess was what the registry had done to him at fourteen.
   194	
   195	He knew three things about it that were not guesses, and he counted them. It had been there before Oryn; session nine was five years old and she was two months. It had been there before Brom's read caught it, because a read catches what is already there. And it was not the Quiet — the Quiet was outside him, a line on the ground that stopped Paths, and this was inside, and the fragments went round it.
   196	
   197	He took his hands off himself, and felt, from his own side, the shape of the reading going, as he had felt hers go, and then that was gone too, and he was a man on a grain sack in a loft with five people breathing round him and a healer's condition met.
