   116	
   117	The healer had named the hour herself. She had woken with the sun and eaten the sixth of a loaf that lay by her head, and slept again, on purpose, for two hours more; then she had taped Lira's foot without a word, and sat down against a pannier with her eyes on the top of the sky. She would do her two when it was straight up, she said. They had all told her that it was the hour at which those animals had never been seen about, and if she was to shout twice more she would do it then.
   118	
