editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md-229-Two lanterns. The painted crosses and arrows going away across the planed floor into the half-dark. The frames along the east wall on their heavy springs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md-230-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md-231-Cael walked out into the middle of the floor and stood there, and shut his eyes, and let it look.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md-232-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:233:He had never let it look before. Since the road he had spent every waking hour answering it, *no* and then names, a word a thing at every threshold, so that it would stop asking. He had never once stood still and let it ask about everything at once and not answered any of it, only listened to the asking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md-234-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md-235-It was like opening a window on a night full of small sounds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md-236-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md-237-The hall came to him as places where things were resting. The frames along the east wall stood on their feet on the boards, and each foot was a place, a small sure point where weight met wood and stayed. The lantern on its hook by the rail hung, and its hook in the beam was a point, and the point was asking. The bench Lira sat on had four legs, and the legs were four points in a square. The bar across the foot of the gallery stair was two points and a line. The great door had its hinges, three of them, all points, and along its edge, where it stood open against nothing, a long empty place that the new thing leaned toward with its whole attention, the way a dog leans toward a door with somebody behind it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md-238-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md-268-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md-269-Brom moved his weight in the doorway. Cael felt it before he heard it: a rest lifting, and another place on the boards, half a pace to the left, where a foot was about to come down, lit faintly a breath before the foot came. He opened his eyes in time to see Brom's boot come down there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md-270-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md-271-"It knows where you're going to stand," he said. "Before you stand there. A breath early, not more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md-272-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:273:Karis wrote it down without a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md-274-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md-275-The door was Brom's idea.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md-276-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md-277-He had stood in the doorway all this while with his right hand on the edge of the open door, because somebody had to be in the door, and he had noticed something that Cael had not.
