editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-10-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-11-Seln nodded once. He put his hands flat on the table and did not move them again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-12-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-13-So Cael began at Denvash.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-14-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:15:He told the circle chalked on the worn boards of a district office, and the warden-adjunct three feet away watching the brass plate let into her desk, and the thing that woke behind his breastbone and came up to the edge of his sight. He told the eleven seconds he had counted while the plate did not light, and the one word that came at the end of them, very far away and very close, and the light at the edge of his sight going out like a pinched wick and never coming back when he looked. He told the letter that had made Denvash impossible for Hesk's sake, and the road east. He told Lira next, because Lira had come next: six weeks of bouts in a yard at Ardenmere, with her knocking him down on the boards between them more times than either of them had counted. Then one cold night on a cot under a window, with the Log on his knee, something had been written in the dark place behind his breastbone where the light had gone out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-16-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-17-"I didn't know what it was," he said. "I sat and read it twice. Then the first time I spent it on a floor, Lira saw it, and she knew what it was before I did, because it was hers."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-18-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-19-"It was mine," said Lira, from the end of the bench. "I'd know my own burst in a dark room."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-20-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-21-He told the others in their order. The Bronze from Ardenmere, who had driven him across a floor with flat hands until something of the push came into his right shoulder and stayed there. Brom and his three mornings, and the read that came out of them, the cheapest thing Cael owned and the one he trusted most. Reydan, on a scored floor, and the give that took a blow into the body and kept it from going further. Karis, at Greyvane, across a formal yard in the cold, with her little fires coming up out of the ground. Hers was the only one he had ever asked for first, and she had said yes in writing, and been asked again the night before he spent it, and said yes again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-22-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-23-He told the notices.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-24-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:25:"They come after, every time," he said. "Words behind my eyes, as if somebody were holding up a card: *fragment acquired*, and the name of the Path with *adjacent* after it, because it isn't the Path, it's a piece of how the Path is done. Then how long it lasts and how far in it goes, and sometimes a tier, and then how it was taken and what kind of engagement it was." He looked at the cloth. "I don't know who writes them. Nobody's ever been able to tell me, and I've stopped asking out loud. I write them down word for word, every time, and I've never once found one wrong."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-26-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-27-Seln did not move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-28-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-29-He told the rule: how the four of them had sat at a long table at Halcenvane, before the stair, and written it in a minute in Karis's hand, and numbered it, and signed it. Nothing made to happen. Only inside a fight somebody else begins. The cost on paper first. And after Norhold, a fourth line under the three, initialled by four: never again in the middle of a fight unless what follows has been priced first, at the weight it will come at.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-30-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-31-"Because every one of them costs," he said. "The Wind's bill comes to the hip. The push comes out of the right shoulder, four blows a sitting and no more. The read is cheap, and the give is a cup that fills. Karis's spark burns me too, at the fingers, if I spend it past two contacts. The storm's the heaviest thing I own, and when I lay a lane the whole of me goes a beat slow afterward for ten counts, and if I lay three close together the third costs twelve." He said it in the log's voice, each figure exact. "Those are the prices, and they're all in the book. Nothing's free. That was the first thing I learned, on a cot at Ardenmere with the Log on my knee, and it's still true."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-32-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:33:He told Daeva, on the stone at Norhold in front of eight thousand people, and the storm that came out of her into him, and the eleven weeks of it settling, and the morning on the second floor when it settled.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-34-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-35-He told the woman on the Ostrand road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-36-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:37:"And under all of them," he said, "there's one I don't count. Session nine, at fifteen: a piece of something like Tide, from nobody. There was no Tide practitioner within a day's ride. There was nothing at stake and nobody against me, nothing the rule says it needs, and it came anyway." He moved his hand on the cloth. "I don't know what it is. I've kept it out of the count for three years, because the day I count it I've decided something I don't know. I've got three words for it. *Still open. Still real. Patience.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-38-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-39-He stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-40-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-41-He had told it all but one, and he had told it in order, with the prices. His hands were steady. He was tired, too, in a way the ribs had nothing to do with. It was as if he had carried something heavy up four flights and set it down, and only now felt how heavy it had been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-42-
