editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-76-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-77-"Can an instrument read it off you?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-78-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-79-That was the one Cael had thought about longest, over the years, at night, and he answered it carefully.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-80-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:81:"No frame has ever read anything off me but what I put in front of it. The plates in the wing read what I give them, and I give them the band. The read at Brom's grade is a surface, and I can hold a surface. And my own Arbiter went out at Denvash after one word. It's never lit since. I've not stood in a station since."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-82-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-83-"A light that goes out is a reading too," said Seln. "Somebody writes down that it went out."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-84-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-85-Karis looked up from the cloth very sharply. Seln did not look at her; he waited.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-86-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:87:"I know," said Cael. "They wrote down the word it said first. That's all they've ever had."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-88-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-89-"And the last." Seln moved one finger on the wood, very slightly, the first time his hands had moved since he sat down. "Does it ever show where somebody counts? Not in a fight, but after, in a corridor or on a stair or at a table, where a man like me would see it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-90-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-91-"The bills show," said Cael. "The hip, the shoulder. People see a fighter with a bruise and a stiff walk, and they think bruise. Nobody's ever looked at a bruise and thought anything else." He thought of Ephram's three close-ruled pages. "The band shows, and Ephram's charted it for a year, but the band's the thing I chose to show. It breathes because we made it breathe."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-92-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-128-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-129-"I knew there was somebody in the corner on the second night, and I knew who on the third." Seln's voice had not changed at all. "I did not look down the flight. A man in a doorway with a bag looks where the officer looks, so I did not look."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-130-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-131-Nobody in the room moved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-132-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:133:Cael went on, because there was nothing else to do. He told the rule counted on five fingers against Brom's coat: earnest, a man spending everything he had in the light and meaning every word; his, begun by him, before Cael knew there was anything to begin; close, one flight; his work, the wing's officer doing the wing's work; and awake, the little finger. He told how he had not done it the way he did a burst, all at once before thinking could catch up, but slowly, on purpose, on the cold stone, knowing exactly what he did and to whom. And the quiet coming down over him like a heavy door swung shut, the stair's sounds gone, the light gone flat, and three seconds or four of the night going on above him without him in it. And then the notice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-134-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:135:He said it word for word, because he had it by heart and had never once said it aloud.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-136-
