editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-227-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-228-Lira went third, and Cael went with her, because she had said not to help her and he was not going to, and that did not mean he could not be on the same stair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-229-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-230-She went down the top stair the way she had come up it, which turned out to be sitting, one step at a time, with her hands on the tread behind her and her right foot held clear. At the bottom she got up by the newel post and stood on the third-floor landing breathing hard, and looked at him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-231-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:232:"Not a word," she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-233-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-234-"I didn't say anything."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-235-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-236-"You were going to." She put her hand on the newel post's cap, worn smooth by a hundred years of hands. "*Post*," she told it, seriously. "*It holds the rail up. People hold it going down.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md-237-
