editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md-36-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md-37-"Reach, how far a reading goes past the one column the station has for it." She looked at the four slips. "Strength they leave alone, because strength they like. Strength's easy; you write it down in a rank and build somebody a ladder. Daeva was read at thirteen, in a garden court, and nobody ever corrected a line of hers. She's the strongest thing on the continent at her age, and they've never once taken anything off her paper. They built her a ladder to her height and called it a cycle."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md-38-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md-39-Cael sat very still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md-40-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:41:*They did this to me the other way — built the ladder to my height and called it fairness.* He had the letter by heart; it lived inside the back board of Hesk's book, in the satchel across his chest. He had never told Karis its words. She had come to the same sentence from the far side of a case.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md-42-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md-43-"And reach is what they can't have," Karis went on, "because reach is what doesn't fit a column. A child who reads Current and something of Mire isn't a better Current. She's a question the registry hasn't got a box for. So the function closes the question before anybody asks it. Margin reading not to be entered. And she goes home with one family on her paper and grows into the paper, because people do."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md-44-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md-45-"Nine weeks of nothing," said Lira quietly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md-46-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md-50-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md-51-"The second half of the first finding is the mark on Cael's file."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md-52-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md-53-Nobody moved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md-54-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:55:"The leaf says the function is appended to the reading and advises what's entered. It doesn't say what happens when the reading gives it nothing to advise, and nothing else in the case says it in words." She looked at Cael. "But your file says it. The petition put it in schedule two by its full title, on the first night of the year, and you read it. *Suppression-Advisory Watch, Priority Level 4.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md-56-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md-57-"Advisory," said Seln.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md-58-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:59:"Advisory, the same word, on a designation nobody at any height has ever been able to trace to an office." Karis drew a breath. "Cael told me on the third night, when he brought the bread. I asked him about Denvash, and he told me: eleven seconds. The plate lit eleven seconds late, and the word came at the end of them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md-60-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md-61-Cael had not known she had asked for anything but the bread. He remembered telling her, standing at her elbow with the plate in his hand, and her not looking up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md-62-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:63:"Eleven seconds is a long time for a reading," said Karis. "Every other reading on the continent is done before you've finished a breath. I think the function had his reading in front of it for eleven seconds and couldn't find a column to put it in, any column, and couldn't take the margin off because it was all margin. So it did what a good form does when it can't be filled in: it failed safe, and wrote the one word it had for a thing that couldn't be written. Then it marked the file, so that whoever came after would know there was something there it hadn't been able to correct." She laid the slip down. "The mark has no office behind it because no office made it. It made itself, the way a stamp comes down when a lever's pulled. And Ilsev sent it up, at the hearing, twice, to every height she could reach, and asked who'd authorized it. Something answered her each time, something that wasn't anybody."
