editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md-116-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:117:"Under the policy there's something older: the reading, whatever it is that wakes behind your breastbone at Kindling and reads you. The function sits on it. It's older than the function by however long, and the case doesn't say how long, and I don't know." She laid her hand on the cloth. "But the case does show me one thing about what was there before the function came, because the first memoranda had to clear it out of the way. The first three issues, about eleven years apart, in the oldest paper. And they're not about the forty. They're about the old custom."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md-118-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md-119-She looked at Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md-120-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:121:"You know the margin I copied at Greyvane. The one in the old records, where an assessor once looked at somebody whose work went across many families, and couldn't fit him in any column, and wrote him down by what he could show instead, by judgment, with the old word." She did not drop her eyes. "I said it once, at Halcenvane, and closed it as a question. I'll say it once more, here, because I have to, and then not again. *Unbound.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md-122-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:123:The word sat on the table among them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md-124-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:125:"That was the old way," she said. "Before the policy. An assessor who met a reading that went across many families could write that, pending further study, and the person went on with his life under that word, and nobody corrected anything. It was a door the old system left open. And the first three memoranda shut it, in order. Each of them cites an article of the directive by its number, the same directive I sat over at Halcenvane." She counted on her fingers, slowly, one, two, three. "The first: *close the door.* No assessor may designate by judgment of what a person shows; a station enters what the reading gives, and only that. The second: *remove the exit.* The provisional custom, pending further study, is struck; there is to be no designation that waits on study. The third: *delete the name.* The old word is discontinued, and every person who carried it is to be re-entered under a flag."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md-126-
