editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:227:He had been kept by that sentence. He knew it as surely as he knew the ribs. Two years of true, empty weeks had gone up out of the wing with his name on the outside fold, and every one of them had said that nothing had happened. That sentence had stood between him and the people who read returns. And here was the same sentence, word for word, clipped to the back of a girl from a river town. In her case it said that the correction had held. Nothing to report. She had gone home smaller and stayed there, and nine weeks of somebody's careful watching had found nothing in her worth writing down, because the thing worth writing down had been taken off her paper before she was old enough to know it was there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md-228-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:229:The same words. One set had kept him, and the other had buried her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md-230-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md-231-He did not say that either. He put his hand on the edge of the table, and Lira, without looking at him, put her own hand over it and took it away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md-232-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:233:They read eleven more pairs before anybody stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md-234-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md-235-Nobody had decided how many to read. Karis read records and Brom read orders, and after a while Lira took orders too, so that Brom could rest his hand. The voices went on across the bare cloth in the circle of the lamp, very low. *Reads into. Something of. Second family, faint. Enter. Margin reading not to be entered.* Station after station went by, a mill town and a ferry crossing and a district with a name Cael had once seen on a map, a girl and a boy and a girl, and on every back the narrow slip, the nine weeks, the nothing.
