editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:245:Karis looked at her a moment, and gave it to her. Lira unfolded the outer sheet on her knee and smoothed it with the side of her hand. It was Seln's copying hand again, but the copying hand had done something Cael had never seen it do: it had kept the shape of another man's writing inside its own. The lines ran uphill and the capitals were too large. The spelling was a careful man's spelling, a man who wrote seldom and had looked up the hard words.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md-246-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md-247-Lira read it aloud.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md-248-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md-249-"*To the clerk of the district office. Sir. I am the father of the girl entered at the river station on the ninth. I do not write to complain and I hope you will not take it so. At the frame the keeper told us she read strong and read into a second family besides, and he said he had not seen it often, and shook my hand. Now the paper has come and it says only the one, and the first rank. I expect there has been an error in the copying, which I know is easy done. I do not mind which it is. She is a good girl either way. Only she asks me, and I do not know which to tell her. If you would write and say which, I would be grateful. Your servant.*"
