editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:33:The memoranda were heavier than anything else on the table, and Karis turned the first stack over before she touched a word of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md-34-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md-35-"Bottom first," she said, "always. The top of a pile is what somebody wanted you to find."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md-36-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md-37-So she started at the oldest, at the yellow end, and worked forward. The sheets were printed, all of them, each a single leaf in a registry circular's form, with a number at the head and a seal pressed at the foot and a line of signatures under the seal. Cael watched her turn them, and the paper changed as she went, from a thick soft yellow stock that had gone brown at its edges to a thinner whiter one, and then to the hard bright paper the registry used now, the paper of the instrument on his own sill. The type changed too. The oldest leaves had heavy letters with long tails, the kind on the founders' volumes Karis kept on her top shelf, and the newest had the narrow even type of every form Bracken had ever put across a counter. The seal at the foot of each changed by small degrees, a ring of letters gone and another come, the river and the scale drawn first one way and then another. The signatures changed with every issue, officer after officer, a new name every few leaves.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md-38-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:39:The words did not change.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md-40-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md-41-Karis read the heading of the oldest aloud, and then the heading of the newest, and then four from the middle at random, and they were all the same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md-42-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:43:"*Memorandum to keepers of stations and to clerks of entry. Corrective classification at Kindling evaluation.*" She put her finger under the line on the newest leaf. "And the body. It's three sentences, and it's been three sentences since this one." She touched the yellowest. "A word changes here and there, and *shall* became *is to*. A clause about the post came in when the post did. Otherwise you could lay the oldest on top of the newest and read straight through them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md-44-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md-45-"How many?" said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md-46-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md-47-"Nineteen issues, reissued every ten years or so, by the look of the seals, when the officers changed or the paper did. Nineteen times somebody sat at a desk and had it set in type again, and signed it, and sent it to every station in the Compact."
