5:"I was fourteen. It was a district office on a cold morning, with a chalk circle on the boards for me to stand in. Something woke behind my breastbone, as it wakes in everybody on that day: a small light at the edge of my eye. You'd call it an Arbiter." He kept his voice low and his breath slow, as she had taught it to go. "It looked at me for eleven seconds, and I counted them. There's a plate let into the desk in those offices, which lights with the name of your Path. For eleven seconds it stayed dark."
35:"And this morning."
37:"This morning there's a tenth." He kept his eyes on her face, which hung over him upside down and grey. "It wants a real thing done to me for a long time, at a cost, by somebody who means it, and you had told me at Oxhollow what the long reading was: both hands, and all of you, for minutes. I knew when I lay down what it was likely to be. At the end there was a place in me standing open, and the thing that you read with was at the door. I could have kept that door shut. I'm better at shutting them than at anything else I do. I opened it."
59:"There's a leaf," said Cael. "I wrote it last night in the loft, before I had so much as crossed your yard. Everything that I knew is on it, and what I had made up my mind to do. You ought to read it before you answer, since it's worse than what I've told you and it's truer."
79:"That's the hundredth."
85:"Since last night. He had the four of us up the ladder and told us."
89:"That it was wrong." Brom's eyes stayed on her and kept away from the plank. "Each of us, one after another, in our own words. He said we were right. He said the right thing was to come across this yard and knock, or to tell you this morning before you'd washed your hands, and that he would do neither, and that he'd tell you the minute it was over."
109:"I've one rule at this table that I never taught anybody, since I'd not have thought it wanted teaching. Nobody lies down here without being told what I'm going to do to them, and why, and what it's like to cost. I tell a child. I tell a man who's out of his head with a fever, and I say it twice, in case one of the times gets in." She nodded at the plank. "You lay down there this morning knowing what you were going to do to me. The one person in this room who wasn't told was the healer. You turned my table round. That's what you did, and I'll thank you not to dress it."
113:"You let me walk in here with my purse open. I'd put a day on my round for this. I've wanted very few things for myself since I crossed the Line, and this was one of them, and you knew that it was; you watched me want it at a ford." Her voice had not risen. It had gone lower, and each word came out separately. "You used it. You lay there like a man asleep and you let me spend."
123:He lay under that. He had known for a night and a morning what she would be entitled to say, and he had supposed that knowing it would be some sort of armour. It was not any sort.
137:"I've spent a mending. I knew that I would; a long one costs that, and I'm no emptier than a long one leaves me." She unfolded her arms and looked at her palms. "Past that I can't tell you, since I can't read myself. No Tide can. So I'll learn it as I learn everything. There are five on that bench after noon, and the first of them will tell me whether these are what they were. If they come back short, you'll hear it from me before you hear anything else today."
149:"I know what you meant. You meant to be square. You'd take whatever I gave you and call the account shut." She shook her head, once. "You don't set it. That's the first of what it costs. You're not the one who knows the price, and you'll go about not knowing it until I do, which won't be today."
153:"And here's the second. You wrote that the real reason was that you didn't know what I'd say. I know what I'd have said. I knew it by the fourth line of that leaf." Her eyes were level and tired, and there was no heat in them at all. "It was mine to say, last night, through a door, and you took the saying. So I'm keeping the answer. You'll not have it today, and you'll not have it when you're old. You can carry that with the other ten."
175:"You've been a thing that nobody could read since you were fourteen. I've stood at that door five times and once more for twenty minutes, and I've a list to keep, and I came all the same." For the first time that morning he heard something in her voice that was not arithmetic. "I want to know what you find, and I want to be the one you tell."
189:It was still the hundredth morning, and the sun had not yet reached the well, when Oryn looked down and saw that she was holding a book.
199:Nobody had ever before told her the truth a night too late, in writing.
207:She had not said that part aloud. She had taken it for tiredness. In a long reading the healer goes along the patient and comes home to her own left hand, over and over, and toward the end she begins to hear herself as a person hears her own boots on a road at night. This had been like hearing a second pair. They had fallen exactly with hers, with the same short step on the right, where her heel was raw.
217:So perhaps it had taken nothing that could be weighed. He had told her that it never showed. She believed him; he had been at some pains, that morning, to tell her things that did him no good.
221:She gave herself away every day. That was all that her trade was. She found where a stranger's current was standing behind a break, and opened the way with her own, and held it open while six weeks of knitting went through in an hour; and she paid for the six weeks. An ox-driver had a foot today because she had been a little older by evening. She had never grudged one of them. It did not come from nowhere, and she had always known where it came from, and had chosen each time to spend it.
227:She had known it by the fourth line of his leaf, as she had told him, and she did not say it over now, even to herself, since a thing said over in an empty room is half given away. She turned the keeping of it over once instead, to be certain that she had her reasons straight and was not merely being hard. If the answer had been yes, then to tell him so was a pardon. If it had been no, it was a sentence, and a person can serve a sentence and be done. Either way he would have had from her, a day late, the very thing that he had gone round her to avoid, which was an end to not knowing. He had made her answer into a thing that never happened. It could stay so, and he could do his own carrying.
237:She had put him first on purpose. It was the worst thing on the bench, and it was a thing that she knew: a scythe cut across the front of the leg, as old as the harvest, which had never closed because he had never once sat down. She had him on the plank where a boy had lain that morning. She put both hands round the shin, above the place and below it, and shut her eyes.
245:"That's one," said Oryn. "And you'll sit down tonight, or I'll have it open again myself."
251:The girl was sitting on the well's coping, with her sling, and her foot set flat on the stones. She had been there since noon. She had looked at nobody who went in at that door or came out of it, and she had missed nothing.
267:She went back to her room. She had a bench to pay that was owed five days now, and a round behind it that was owed a good many more; and a boy in that hold had a piece of her craft in him that he did not know how to use. She was hungry to hear what it would find, and she did not take that for forgiveness. She had been hungry to read him for sixteen days, and it had not made her fond of his ribs.
271:It was an hour short of noon on that same day when Cael first heard it, and he was at the trough.
277:He ate it standing, and tasted none of it. Nobody came to him. Brom looked across once from the bench under the window, and got up, and climbed the ladder to the loft. On the platform by the western gate a lean man was watching the valley, as he did at every hour of every day. Cael had been left alone with a bowl by four people who knew when a person was not fit to be spoken to; and when the bowl was empty he went to break the ice for the geese, since it wanted doing, and since it could be done by somebody who was not thinking.
279:The trough stood by the barn wall, with a skin on it as thick as a slate. He put the heel of his right hand through it, and it was the left that he looked at afterward, turning it over in the cold: the feeling had come back as far as the knuckles in the night, and a hound's weight had dwindled to a yellow mark along the bone. That bill was nearly paid. He had come into this yard with a good many, and one by one a healer had told him that they were done.
285:He knew that beat. He had tried for four days down a mountain to walk at a healer's pace, and had failed, since he went in fits, as he had always gone.
299:He took his hands out of the water and dried them on his shirt. A healer was sitting on a stool across the yard, behind a shut door, with his leaf on her knee and an answer in her that he would never be given. He had nothing that he could do about either. He had four people in a loft who had told him the truth the night before and had not slept on it, and he owed them the morning, in order, with nothing left out.
3:They had not gone to their blankets, and they had not gone down to the yard. They were sitting among the barley sacks where he had left them the night before, in very nearly the same places, with a shutter propped open for the light and two torn leaves still lying on the boards.
17:"She said that I'd turned her table round. Nobody lies down on it without being told what she means to do to them, and this morning the one who wasn't told was the healer." He made himself give them the rest of it as she had given it to him. "She said the part that she minded most was the four of you. I hadn't done it without knowing its name. I'd been told the name by four people, and agreed."
21:"I'd priced it for her on the leaf. Whatever it cost me, down to her going north without me. She won't have that. She says a patient doesn't hand her his own fine ready counted, and that I'm not the one who knows the price, and shan't be until she does." He drew a breath. "And she knows what she'd have said last night, if I had knocked. She's keeping it. I'm not to be told, today or ever."
29:"She's set two things on it. I'm to use it on myself before I use it on anybody. And whatever I find, she is the one I tell, on a day that she names." He looked round the four of them. "So if there's anything to find, it's hers before it's yours. I'm saying so now, in order that nobody should think later that I kept a thing back without leave. It's part of what's owed."
39:It was Karis. The words came out of her like a gate swinging on a dry hinge, each with a creak in the middle of it, and all four of them turned to look at her. She had spoken three words on the day before. She touched her throat, and frowned at it, and went on regardless, since it was plain that she had been saving this up since first light.
47:He understood her, and he saw that the others did. She had spent two years ruling a leaf about what he was, from the outside, with a curve on it and a column, and she had wanted the result of that morning more than she had ever wanted a result; she had written so against herself in the dark. Now there was a thing in him that read how people were built, and she would never ask him to turn it on a wall, or on a stranger, or on herself. She had put that down, in the only voice that she had, before she could so much as begin to wish it.
55:"It's the hundredth," he said, and held his hand out to Karis, palm upward.
57:She gave him the leather sleeve without a word. He counted what was in it, touching each iron head with his thumb: there were five. Then he took down the bow from the peg where it had hung since the ninety-ninth evening and looked along it, as he would have looked along an axle.
59:"There's a hook for this in her near pannier. I'll ask her for it when she's on her feet, and not before; she's had enough taken off her this morning." He hung the bow on his shoulder. "And the carter went down light at first light, with Karis's four lines in his hat for the one-armed man, and the hold's mark under them. So that's gone, and it says six."
63:He did not sleep that night, and he had not expected to.
65:He lay between two sacks with Hesk's book under his hand. Lira had brought it up the ladder at dusk inside her coat, and with it one sentence, said exactly as a healer had said it: that it was a true page, and that true was not the same as paid. He had written nothing in it since.
69:His own current went along at the edge of every thought, quick and slow. He had had it since the trough, and he had begun to know its ways. It quickened when he thought of a table. It dragged when he was tired. And somewhere toward the middle of the night he noticed that it was not one sound, and that it never had been; he had only wanted quiet enough to tell.
73:Wind was the first, since Lira was asleep six feet from him and it had always leaned her way. It was a dry hiss, like the pale grass of the Fallow going over in a breath of air, and it lived in his feet. The read came after, low and even, under his soles: a millstone turning, heard through a floor. Something ticked in his right hand, as a lamp-wick ticks when the oil is poor, and he knew whose fire that was; and under his breastbone Pressure drew and filled, slowly, like a bellows worked by a tired arm that has a long day's iron still in front of it.
79:The ninth he knew by what it took away. At dusk it had lifted, as it lifted every dusk, and he had told it no; and he had heard, for the first time in two years of telling it so, what he was refusing, since every other sound in him had dropped as it came up, as a yard goes quiet under falling snow.
83:He lay and counted them, and there were nine. It was like a room he had lived in for eighteen years with the lights suddenly on. Every stick in it was where he had always known that it was. He had crossed that floor ten thousand times in the dark without barking a shin; and he had never once seen it.
103:"The first night I thought I'd go mad. A forge doesn't go quiet when you bank it. The iron ticks while it cools, every piece at its own rate, and the coals settle, and the hide of the bellows creaks as it dries. There's water dripping off the tongs into the tub. I lay on a heap of sacks and I could have told you which sound was which with my eyes shut. I had the lot by heart inside an hour, and I never slept a wink."
105:"And the second night?"
107:"The same, near enough. The third I slept like a stone." He rubbed the four dark lines on his fingers with his thumb. "I asked the old man who swept for us how that was, for nothing in the shed had got any quieter. He said you never stop hearing a forge. You stop listening to it. And the day it makes a noise that you don't know, he said, you'll be on your feet with a bucket before you've woken up."
111:"So I'm to wait for the third night."
113:"I'd not know. I'm telling you about a shed." Brom got his legs up onto the boards. "But I'd leave off counting them, for a start. You know that there are nine, and you knew it yesterday. They'll still be nine when you wake."
119:The grain came on the hundred and first morning, in three carts from the river country, and by the middle of the forenoon it was going up the ladder past the place where they slept.
121:It was the last of the year's. The carters had come up through the stretch at noon the day before and slept in the cart-yard, three heavy men in sheepskins, with frost on their mules' eyelashes; and they wished to be unloaded and gone before the roads grew any harder. Holds farther up the valley would fetch that barley through the whole of the cold term, a cart at a time. Until then it lay in Thornwater's loft, under Thornwater's thatch, which was all that a grain hold was for.
131:He had his left hand on the stone rim and the dipper in his right. He heard it begin, and that was new; it had shifted in him before, on a bluff and on a road and in a loft two nights ago, and each time he had known of it only as a ship's passenger knows that the cargo has slid. Now he heard the whole of it. The low sound rose, and went over from one side of him to the other in a long slow roll, as a sea goes over a bar, and it took as long about it as a person takes to draw a bucket. Then it lay down again, and where it lay was a little lower than before, and a little to one side.
135:He had been afraid of that weight since the day it came to him, and had lain awake more than once over what it might do if it ever rolled when he was not ready. He stood at a well with a dipper in his hand and heard it settle, and all that he felt was a kind of respect.
139:It was there. His boot came down on the frozen earth of the yard as it had come down all morning. But he felt it come down half a beat after it had done so, as a man hears an axe from across a valley after he has watched it fall.
161:He knew what she meant. It had moved twice in one week on a bluff, for a quarter of an hour and then for forty minutes; and nobody had tried to hit him during either.
169:It was her drill, or it was the drill that an instructor had built for her on a bluff: three lies and a truth. The front foot went first, and then the back, and then the shoulders; and on the fourth count there was a step where a burst would have been. He had walked it beside her for two years.
171:He had not asked that thing for anything since a rut on a hill, and not once in a hundred days for practice. But this was a yard, at noon, inside a fence, where a healer mended with her door open and a researcher might light a lamp; and if he was to be half a beat behind himself on some road next week, he would sooner learn what that was like here, among geese.
193:"You've always been early. You load it on the three." She showed him, sitting, with a small turn of her one free shoulder. "I saw it the first week in that lane. I never said, for it worked. You'd have it ready, and then you'd go, and you never once went late. I thought it was how you did it."
233:"Eleven," she said. "One on your face. Two late and two early. Six right." She let her breath go. "I walked that count for a month on a new leg, with a man at the rail who'd have struck me for a burst. He never once told me to wait for the floor."
239:He sat down beside her on the coping. His calves ached pleasantly, and the thing under his soles was still half a beat behind, and it no longer troubled him. He had spent a hundred days holding nine things shut; and until this noon he had never been able to hear what any one of them cost while he was spending it. A bill had always come afterward, in a forearm or a rib. This was the first time that he had been shown the price beforehand.
241:On the platform by the western gate the bowman had turned round. It was the one hour of the day when nothing had ever come up that valley, and he seemed to keep it for looking inward, at the hold. He had watched a boy fall about a yard eleven times, and get it right, and he stood with his bow at the length of his arm and gave no sign that it was any business of his. Then his eyes went to the girl on the coping, who had seen a fault from thirty feet with one arm tied, and they stayed there a good deal longer.
245:*Hundred and first day. The yard, at noon, inside the fence. Wind, eleven leans of a foot each, to learn it with the ground late. That is practice, and I am not putting it in the number; the number is what I spend on a road, or in a fight, or on a person. I enter it here so that nobody has to take my word for what's left out.*
253:She had been at it since the middle of the day, with the door of the back room standing open, and he had counted four go in and come out: a carter with a wrenched back, and a woman of the hold, and two that he did not know, who had walked in with the grain. She stopped her stride and a half from the coping, wiping her hands on her breeches. Lira had gone up to the loft by then, on her right hand and both knees.
255:"I did five yesterday," said Oryn. "Two that I mended and three that I told. Nothing came back short. They're what they were."
