343:"Sixty tomorrow," said the bowman. "He'll move the stake."
3:The fourth finding was not about stone, and Karis gave it on the hundred and eighth morning in a different voice from the others.
13:"One. A bar of iron of the registry's pattern, bearing its stamp and the number thirty-one, stands on the edge of the Quieting at the Fallow Ring, a boot's width from the place where I found that edge with my feet. Two. By its rust, and by the turf grown against it, it has stood there about five years. Three. It was driven at the far end of a measure, so that whoever drove it had found the same circle that I paced, and had a cord or a chain to find it with. Four. I do not know what the number counts, or what was written down afterward, or where it is kept."
15:She lowered the leaf. It was the shortest thing that she had read out in six days, and she had plainly cut it down from something a good deal longer; there was not an *as though* in it, or a *we suppose*, and every noun in the four sentences was a thing that somebody in that barn had stood over.
19:"*About five years*," said Seln. "I said five, with a cold term to either side of it, and at the stake I said that it might be six. I should put *five or six*. It reads a little surer than I am."
25:"It is not in the four because nobody in this barn has seen it but one person, once, as a boy, when he was counting corners. I have his memory entered where he gave it, on the leaf of the ninety-seventh, with *entered as said* beneath." Karis touched the roll of thin paper that had the ring of charcoal on it. "That ring lies on mine. It is the best thing that I have to hang a guess on, and I am not promoting a guess to a finding because I should like it to be one."
29:He had been ruling a line through that iron since a wash on the road east. There had been a query sent down to a registry, asking which way certain people had gone; and a paper under a broker's seal, with a sum written twice; and a stake. At a bar of iron eleven days ago he had seen for the first time which way along that line he was looking. He had expected to find the whole of it on her leaf. It was not there. There was a stamp, and a number, and a length of time, and a measure; and under those she had written, plainly, the three things that she did not know.
43:"I have thought of very little else for two nights." Karis rolled the leaf and tied it. "And I shall go on not writing it down."
45:Seln had not moved from the trestle's end. His hand had gone, as it went a dozen times a day, to the strap across his chest, where a locked case hung that he had carried shut on every road past the Line.
47:"There is a thing that I should add, and it is not for the box," he said. "You have put *I do not know where it is kept*. That is exact. I should only like it remembered in this barn that it is kept; and that six days ago a boat went west, to tell whoever keeps it that a crew walked into that ring and out again."
59:It was the evening of the hundred and ninth day, with the light gone from the shutter and the hold's lantern standing on the trestle among the chips. She had asked the four of them to stay down after the day's slips were counted; and when they had settled, on kegs and sacks and the foot of the ladder, she let a good while go by before she spoke. She had three leaves laid side by side on the scrubbed board, and a fourth beside them, which was the rubbing of the founding stone.
65:"There is no fifth. That is what I kept you for." She did not sit down. "I have been laying leaves side by side since before it was light on the morning that I gave you the name, and I have known since then what they say when they lie so."
85:Cael had put no question to her in four days. He had seen a pen held over a clean page at first light, four days ago, from the fourth rung of a ladder, and a leaf put away with nothing on it; and he had understood then that there was a thing which she would sooner sit up with than hand to him.
103:He had had the same three in a row since a far lip, and had looked at them on a good many nights, and he had never heard them said. It was a different thing to hear them in that small exact voice, in a cold barn that smelled of hens, from the one person he knew who had never in her life written down a thing that she had not counted.
125:The healer read the arm at first light on the hundred and tenth day, in the yard, with the frost still white on the well.
127:It was the coldest morning that they had yet had at that gate. The trough had frozen through to the stone in the night, so that Brom had broken it with the back of his hedging bill, and every breath in the yard went straight up. Lira was on the coping, where she had sat at that hour for ten mornings, with her foot set flat and her right hand shut on her knee.
131:He had done the sum on the ladder, and he did it again now, since he could not help it. If the eighth mark had turned, there would be a mending to pay for it, and then more days of watching on top of that. Every one of them would come off a round that was already sixteen behind, and be laid against a girl who had been told that she was not to carry them and would carry them regardless. If it had not turned, a healer went north in the morning.
137:Lira let out a breath that she seemed to have been holding for four days.
139:"All eight. That last one's caught up with the others. It was slow, and it isn't slow now." Oryn drew the wool up and tied it. "That changes nothing that I said at the stone. You've a fortnight behind you, and the rest to do. Nothing in that hand till I say."
149:"That bone's a month old today. I said a month, on the mountain. It may run."
155:She went round the rest of them at her stride and a half, in the time that it took to fill a bucket. She turned Brom's hand to the light and gave it back to him. She laid two fingers on Karis's throat, and told her that she had her voice, and that she was not to lecture with it for a week. To Seln, who had not been hurt since he crossed the Line and seemed half ashamed of it, she gave the nod that she gave to a man of her own trade.
161:"You I can tell nothing. You'd know before I would." She rubbed her palms together. "I go in the morning. That's sixteen out of this gate. A round never gives a day back; it takes one. So I'll call it seventeen at Oxhollow, where there's a table that's been looking for me since the hundred and fourth."
163:He had the sum before she had finished it: the eighty-fourth day at a ford, and twenty days of a round, and seventeen on top.
165:"That's the hundred and twenty-first."
171:She settled the matter of the mule before the middle of the morning, and she settled it as she settled the order of a bench.
173:Cael had not seen that it was a matter. He had known for eleven days that three boxes stood in that loft at a hundredweight each, that the carter who had brought them east was gone home with grain, and that the ferry was two days off. He had put all of it where he put the things that would be dealt with when there was leisure, behind a table and a birthday and a week of slips.
183:"He'd carry one. He'd say it was nothing, and he'd be grey by noon."
185:"So that's two boxes on two backs, and one left over, and a case that the quiet one won't put down." She counted it on her fingers. "And a girl who's to fall on her face sooner than put a hand out, on a road that's ice in the ruts. Who steadies her, when the pair of you have a hundredweight apiece?"
195:"I've had her thirty rounds, and she's a mule. She'd as soon carry boxes as panniers. Boxes don't want mending at the end of the day." The healer spoke more quickly than she was used to, and looked at none of them. "There's a beast at every gate on my round. They feed mine, and one of them shoes her. The next has a jenny that's eaten her head off since the barley was in and done nothing for it. I'll have that one off them, and they'll thank me. You've nobody who'd lend you so much as a goat."
201:He shut his mouth, since it was the second time in ten days that he had been told that he was not the one who set a price.
219:"I rode her down a mountain for four days," she said, "and I wouldn't say that she'd an eye. I said that she was a saddle."
227:The stake was moved to sixty paces at noon, and half of Thornwater found that it had business near the western gate.
231:Cael watched from the wicket, with Brom's shoulder beside him and the valley open in front. The sack hung a long way off in the frozen grass. Beyond it, at very nearly twice the distance, stood a white stone that a lean man had set in his first year on that walk.
241:"I know that it's left. It was left on the hundred and fourth."
255:He could not see her. He could see a strip of grey blanket through a gap between two planks, lying across her breast, and it did not stir. He thought of a girl who had run a quarter-mile of that fence flat out and stopped dead on both feet. He thought of the same girl a hundred yards down a ledge with her arms out and a herd coming, who had never in her life done a thing slowly if there was a way to do it fast.
277:It was dusk when Cael went up the ladder to her.
279:He had had the order of it for fifteen days. He had settled it on the evening that five people stopped at a rise and he did not: there was a thing that he would have to say, when he had it, and Lira was to hear it first, and after her the rest. He had not kept to that. He had told a healer the oldest part of it on a table, since that debt would not wait. But the part that was Lira's he had carried past a stone, and a rut, and ten nights in a loft; and she had let him carry it.
281:She was alone on the walk, and that was new. The lean man had gone down to his supper while there was still light in the west, which Cael had never known him do; and he had left a girl with one arm sitting against the southern post, and the yard bow lying spanned on the planks beside her knee. She had the whole valley in front of her, grey going to blue, with the frost coming down on a mile of pale grass and a white stone standing in it; and it came to him that in thirty years nobody but one man had sat where she was sitting at that hour.
291:He gave it to her in order, and low, and he began by telling her that it had not started on a white floor. It had begun at noon on that day, a cord north of the grey stone, while five people were looking at what they had lost and got back; he had opened the read a pace short of the line, and walked, and it had run across the stride without a break. He had gone twenty strides in, to a boulder, and asked for a foot of Wind.
307:"I did that sum the next night, on my back," she said. "I'd have ruled the same. I'd have been sick, doing it."
321:He had not known how much he had been waiting for somebody to say that it was enough. He had carried a half-made sentence for fifteen days as a thing to be ashamed of, since it had no end to it; and she had taken it out of his hands, and looked at it, and told him that a half was what there was.
331:"What was it like?" she said presently. It was her old voice, the one that she had kept for the rail of a practice floor. "When you let them go, in the lane. You'd held them a hundred days. Was it heavy? Did they come slow?"
333:He thought about it honestly, since she was the one person who would know if he did not. He went back to a lane in the dusk, with his feet set as she had taught him to set them and nine things coming up in him at once; and he found the word for it on a sprung oak floor, a long way west, at the end of a week of walking a count.
335:"It felt like a Third-day."
339:"There's one thing more," he said. "I read myself, on the night of the birthday. I found something. I can't give it to you. It's the healer's to hear before anybody; that was her price, and I'm paying it."
347:"Tonight," said Cael.
3:The healer came up the loft ladder that night for the first time, and looked at a wall of barley with a row of blankets under it.
11:Cael gave it to them standing. He gave it as he had given it to one person on a gate-walk an hour before, in the same order and in fewer words: the noon of the ninety-sixth, and a read that had run across a stride, and a foot of Wind at a boulder; a row for a table that had been two true halves; and that he did not know why any of it was so.
13:"Lira's had that since dusk. The rest of you have it now. It's fourteen days late, and I'm sorry for every one of them." He looked at the square woman by the ladder. "And there's one thing more that I have, from the night of the birthday, which none of you is getting. I read myself, as she set it. What I found is hers before it's anybody's. I'm telling you that it exists, so that nobody learns later that I sat on it."
21:"Somebody had to give one. I was the only person there who knew that it was a different ruling for me." He made himself go on. "I spent a quarter of an hour that I'd said I wouldn't, and I let six people stand at a den believing that all six were alike. If I had told you at the stone, at noon, you'd have planned for it. We'd have been in that lane by two hands, and Lira would have two arms."
27:"I have a row to amend," she said. Her voice was whole. "*Stated by him, not tested by me* stands, since it is still true. I am adding beneath it: *tested by him, at noon on the 96th, unwitnessed; told on the 110th*. And now I want something that I have never had. I have had that hillside for fourteen nights in five heads, and not one account of it on paper. I want what each of you saw. In the order in which you saw it. Nothing that you supposed."
53:Her eyes came to Cael then, and rested on him for the length of a breath. He had watched her cross a grey stone eight times at dawn, alone, and had shut a book on it; and he saw that she knew he had kept it shut.
65:"So that's what there is," said Brom. He was sitting on the boards with his knees up, and he went through it on his fingers, as the healer went through a round. "Five that stop and one that doesn't. A floor that nobody can put a year to. A stake." He looked round the lantern. "And there's that clerk's paper. What do they know in the west, when it gets to wherever it's going?"
73:Cael waited for one of them to join those things together. He had sat in a good many rooms where it would have been done before the lamp was trimmed: a seat would have found a word for it, and a clerk would have written the word down, and by morning there would have been a file. Here six people sat round one lantern with a made floor, and an age that had no ruler, and a boy; and each of them left the three lying apart, as Karis had left them on a trestle, since nobody in that loft knew what lay between them and nobody was willing to pretend.
79:She put her finger on the bend, two days east of a square with a dot.
81:"That one I have walked. I have its round, and its radius, and eleven sheets." The finger moved a long way west and south, to a blank among the carter's hill-hatching. "That is a day south of the ferry and a little aside; I rang it on the ninety-second evening, and wrote *guess*." It moved again, far down toward the corner of the hide, where the strokes of his water lay thick. "That is a long way south and east, with no road inside a day of it, and it is a guess as well. And the fourth is not on this skin at all." She lifted her eyes to the loft door. "It is on a sheet that he counted once, as a boy, and cannot place. One sheet had two."
95:"That's how you find a handful. One at a time, for money." She nodded toward Hesk's satchel on its nail, where a broker's cream paper lay folded in a book. "Somebody paid a hundred marks to learn whether a crew would come out of the first, and I do not think that he has stopped wanting to know."
105:[[TIER-B: Oryn to the six in the loft, on her leaving, the packet's partly quoted line beginning "North in the morning…" — coordinator to supply protected text]]
121:Brom had not drawn it up again. He sat at the head of it with his legs hanging into the barn, as he had sat on the first night of the listening, and when Cael came to the edge he went on looking down into the dark where the mule was.
123:"Ten," he said. "I was the third of them, and I've been by you for every one since, or near enough. I've been afraid of a good many things in that time. Dogs. A string. A letter." He rubbed the four dark lines with his thumb. "I went over it tonight, while she was at her wheres, to be sure of my figures. I was never once afraid of you."
135:Cael stood at the loft door for some time afterward, with the frost coming in round his feet and the yard white below him. A boat had gone west on the hundred and second day with a clerk's fair copy in it, and that was eight days ago. He supposed that by now it lay on somebody's desk.
139:Vastin received the broker's completion report as a courtesy copy, forwarded through a registry office that had no interest in any board east of the Line, in the second folder of the morning, marked *Fallow Ring: crew returned, instruments not recovered*. He read it twice — a broker's report from a town on no registry map, on his desk with no routing note — and did not know why it had reached him, and filed that, in the register of a man who had never once in forty years pretended to knowledge he lacked, as the whole of what he knew about it. He returned the copy to its folder unannotated.
155:*The reading turned outward. I ask first. Every time. If I ever don't, that's the day I became the instrument.*
165:He had the next thing ready, and he had had it ready for eight days.
167:"You said that you'd say when, and you go in an hour. I read myself on the night of the hundred and second, as you set it, alone and on my back. I found—"
179:"Once is a morning."
183:"I'd a teacher who struck my knuckles for less than that. You put your hands on a thing one time, and you've learned what sort of morning you were having; you've not learned the thing." Her eyes were level in the grey. "Were you frightened?"
187:"And you've not gone back to it in eight days. I'm not asking you why, since I can see why from here. So you'll go back. You'll read it three times, on three different days, with a night between each. Read it frightened, and read it when you're not, and then you'll know what's there and what was only you." She buckled the strap. "After that you tell me, all of it at once and in order. You don't give me a piece of it now because I'm leaving and you'd like to have paid."
209:*Hers. Three readings of myself, on three different days. Then all of it to her, at once. Nobody on the road, to find out whether I can.*
215:He stayed at the well until the grey had come into the east. Then he turned back sixty leaves, to a line that he had set down under an inn's eave on the forty-ninth night, when he had counted himself free. He read that through, and came forward again to the clean leaf, and wrote the one that had to stand across from it.
217:*Nothing out here reads me. And at a line you can mark with a rock, every Path in this camp stops, and mine doesn't. Karis won't write her hypothesis. I won't write mine. We're going to find the other circles. Pike said nobody goes in twice. Tomorrow makes three.*
223:Oryn went out by the northern wicket as the hundred and eleventh day was breaking, and for the first quarter of a mile she kept putting her hand out for a cheek-strap.
225:It was not there, and she had known that it would not be. She had walked that track thirty times with a small brown head at her right shoulder, and her hand had thirty rounds of habit in it; it went out at every turn of the path, and closed on air, and came back. She let it. A person who has set a great many bones does not expect a habit to knit in a morning.
227:It was a hard grey dawn, with the frost standing an inch deep on the grazing, and her own breath went up in front of her at the pace that she walked. Behind her a hold was waking. She heard the sweep of the well creak, and a goose, and the bar being drawn at the western gate for five people and three boxes.
233:"Sixteen days too long."
239:The track went up through the hold's grazing, past a stone hut, toward a low place in the hill. The goats lifted their heads to see her go by with nothing on a rope. She had a pannier on her own back, with the strap across her chest, and it rode where no pack had ridden since her first year: tape, flask, shears, linen, a cup, a needle, and a loaf. She had the spare rope over her shoulder. It was thirty feet of good three-strand, and she had laid it up herself; and she had carried it for eleven rounds without once needing it.
241:She would need it tomorrow night. There was a jenny at the next gate that was the worst-led beast on seven roads.
243:She did her sum as she climbed. She did it every morning, as another person might say her name over.
245:Out of that gate on the hundred and eleventh, when she had said the ninety-fifth: sixteen. Two days of road to the next. Two gates after it. Then the ford with the oxen, where they had last seen her on the eighty-fourth and had looked for her on the hundred and fourth; she would come down to it on the hundred and twenty-first, if nothing broke, and that was seventeen.
249:She had listened to that chest on the eighty-ninth evening, before she ate, and it had been no worse. They would have looked for her there on the hundred and ninth. That was two days ago already. She would not hear it again until the hundred and twenty-sixth at the soonest.
251:Seventeen days, then, in which she did not know. She had told a crew at a fire what late was. She had not told them that the knowing of it never grew lighter, however often a round came out safe.
263:She slept that night in a carters' halting-ring on the far side of the hill, alone, and she was colder than she had been on any road in years.
265:It was the mule again. She had lain against that warm brown flank on a good many frozen nights and had never once thought of it as a thing that she owned. She lit no fire. It was a road; and besides, she had her back against a chock-stone and a great deal to go over, and she went over it better in the dark.
269:They were as they had been. She had mended eleven people at that gate since a morning on a plank, and every one of them had come up under her palms with banks and a bed. She had laid them on a girl's arm a fortnight old and felt eight places take. Nothing in them was missing. She had told the boy so, since he had a right to know what he had not broken.
277:She had crossed it eight times in the grey, where nobody was to see, and one person had seen and held his tongue for twelve days. She had gone in with her palms up, and with two fingers on her own wrist, and backward, and with her eyes shut; and she had stood in it for a count of a hundred with her arms hanging. Each time it had been the same. On one side of a grey stone she was a Tide, Iron, of the fourth rank, with a reserve like a purse. On the other she was a woman in a dead man's coat who could tie a knot.
279:She had not minded it as much as she had expected, and that was the thing that she had gone in eight times to be sure of. A carter with a split palm did not care which side of a stone her hands had last worked on. And on the evening after that noon she had bound a girl's arm inside the line with a man's folded cuff and strips of her own shirt, having nothing else, and the binding had held until six people could get the arm out to where a Tide was a Tide.
281:She had told a loft that she was no use on the far side of that stone, and it was true. But she had also learned at that stone how long an arm keeps, bound, and how far a person can be carried. It came to about two hundred yards.
289:It had been her teacher's saying, about a single morning. She had heard it over a basin, forty times, with a ruler in the woman's other hand. A reading is a thing that the reader does, on a day, in a temper. The first tells you about the temper. It is the third that begins to tell you about the patient.
293:She wanted that. She looked squarely at the wanting, with her back against a cold stone, as she had once looked at it on a road out of Oxhollow. She had not forgiven him. She did not expect that she would, quite. But she had once been hungry for sixteen days to know how it was that he could not be read, and she was hungrier now to hear what he had found.
295:The road bent in the morning.
297:It had gone north from the wicket, and over the low place, and north again. At the first water it turned along the bank, as a carter's road will, and by the middle of the day she had the sun on her left cheek, and by the afternoon it was in her eyes. The water grew. Another came into it, and a third. There were alders, and then a hedge, and a place where wheels had been in and out of the stream for a great many years.
299:A little before dusk she heard it working.
301:It was a slow heavy knocking, a long way down the valley, with water falling under it. She knew that sound. There would be a wheel at the end of it, and a fence, and a keeper who counted people off a ridge. Beyond the fence there would be a bench, with whoever had sat down on it sixteen mornings ago when her mule should have come in sight.

