editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:61:"One: it is a thing that happens, and I have called it the Quieting. Two: five sigils of five Paths failed at one stride, in one way, and came back so. Three: the floor is older than the oldest stone that I can date. Four: the registry has put iron on the edge of it." She shut the notebook on her finger. "And there is a hypothesis, which is mine, and which is not entered anywhere."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:217:*Nothing out here reads me. And at a line you can mark with a rock, every Path in this camp stops, and mine doesn't. Karis won't write her hypothesis. I won't write mine. We're going to find the other circles. Pike said nobody goes in twice. Tomorrow makes three.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:373:*Still open. Still real. Now I can see it. That's worse. The tenth fragment lets me feel my own currents, and there's one in the place session nine logged, and it isn't the tenth, and it isn't any of the nine. It was there before Oryn. It was there before Brom's read caught it. I don't know what it is. I know where it is now. Patience is over; observation begins.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:13:"One. A bar of iron of the registry's pattern, bearing its stamp and the number thirty-one, stands on the edge of the Quieting at the Fallow Ring, a boot's width from the place where I found that edge with my feet. Two. By its rust, and by the turf grown against it, it has stood there about five years. Three. It was driven at the far end of a measure, so that whoever drove it had found the same circle that I paced, and had a cord or a chain to find it with. Four. I do not know what the number counts, or what was written down afterward, or where it is kept."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:69:"I have a hypothesis and I'm not entering it, because entering it would make it a finding, and it isn't one. It's a fear."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:269:"It's older, I'll say once and not again, than [UNBOUND] — older than the word they retired, and I can't say by how much. Entered as a limit, not a finding. We explained the middle of the story in Ostrand. This is before the beginning, and I don't have a ruler for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:195:*the Quieting*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:199:It was made out of a verb, like the other; it named a thing that went on happening, to whatever came over the line, for as long as it stayed there. It did not say how. It did not say why. It took *the* in front, which made it one thing, and it would take a place after it without complaint: the Quieting at the Fallow Ring. She could write *inside* before it, and *the edge of*, and *a site of*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:203:She distrusted it at once, for the fit, as she would have distrusted a column that came out even at the first adding. A name that sits too well is a hypothesis in a clean shirt, and she had been taught to turn out its pockets. What did the word claim? That something was quieted: she had five rows for that. That it was one thing and not five: she had one stride. It claimed nothing else that she could find, and she went over it twice more to be sure.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:269:"The Quieting." She made a face. "It sounds like what you do to a baby."

