editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:147:He was the hold's boy, twelve or so and all elbows. He put his boot in the stirrup, and a hook on his belt over the string, and straightened his back with a grunt; and the string came up the stock and dropped behind its catch. Then he laid a bolt in the groove. It was a stubby thing with a blunt wooden head and three goose feathers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:149:So that was how it would be. She would never loose a bolt that somebody else had not bent his back for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:165:She loosed. The stock kicked against her collarbone, and the bolt went over the sack by the height of a man and stuck in the frozen grass a long way beyond.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:169:She shot eight. The boy bent his back eight times. Three of them hit the sack somewhere, and not one was in the ring; and at the end of it the boy went out through the wicket in the gate, in the one hour of the day when nothing had ever been seen on that valley, and gathered eight bolts out of the grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:173:She knew what to do with eight bolts. She counted them back into the boy's hand, one at a time, and said that she would be up at noon tomorrow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:179:She had the stock on the rail and her cheek on it before the boy had done with the string.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:123:"Ten," he said. "I was the third of them, and I've been by you for every one since, or near enough. I've been afraid of a good many things in that time. Dogs. A string. A letter." He rubbed the four dark lines with his thumb. "I went over it tonight, while she was at her wheres, to be sure of my figures. I was never once afraid of you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:55:He had had them on him for so long that the cold came in where they had been, at his nape and on the bone, like two doors opened. She stepped back her stride and a half and shook both hands out from the wrist, twice, hard, as he had seen her shake them after a count that found nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:83:"Shut it. Now open it." He did both, and four dark lines went across the pads of four fingers and came back. "It'll draw a string. Did you know what he meant to do on my table?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:149:"I know what you meant. You meant to be square. You'd take whatever I gave you and call the account shut." She shook her head, once. "You don't set it. That's the first of what it costs. You're not the one who knows the price, and you'll go about not knowing it until I do, which won't be today."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:35:She did it six times, without dropping one and without a glance downward, as a person does a thing that she has learned in the dark. When the sixth lay on the stone she hooked the cord round her little finger and drew it tight against her belt, and held the purse out behind her again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:73:Cael had seen the man before without knowing whose he was. He was small and spare, a head shorter than the steading's woman, with a bald brown crown and flour to both elbows; and in all his days at that hold Cael had never heard him say a word, since he had never been near enough to the hearth. Thornwater, it appeared, kept its talk in one place. The woman at the gate counted people with her lips and spent a word on every six of them, and the man on the platform had said perhaps forty in a hundred days; and here was the hold's whole share of speech, walking beside Brom with a wooden spoon in his belt and getting through it at a great rate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:59:"There's a hook for this in her near pannier. I'll ask her for it when she's on her feet, and not before; she's had enough taken off her this morning." He hung the bow on his shoulder. "And the carter went down light at first light, with Karis's four lines in his hat for the one-armed man, and the hold's mark under them. So that's gone, and it says six."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:17:Lira pinned it. She could do that much with one hand: she had a bag of Brom's river pebbles hung at her belt, and she went down on her knees and set one on each corner of the strip without letting the left arm so much as stir in its sling.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:35:She had Lira lift the first strip by its head and hold it to the light of the door. The rubbing stood out white on grey: fine lines, straight and hooked and crossing, a band of them that had gone right round the foot of a stone and met itself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:37:"I do not know that these are letters. I said so on the floor, and nothing has altered. But somebody cut them one stroke after another, with gaps between, and a thing that is cut in groups can be counted in groups." She laid her finger under a hooked stroke with two short bars across its stem. "That. I want to know whether that is on any other strip. If it is on four, it is a kind. If it is on this one alone, it is a scratch, and I shall say so."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:55:"I was wrong about apples." Karis rested her throat, and went on. "I looked into one basket, on one day, and I said *two*. A man who had kept those trees for twenty-six years showed me three. This is one basket." Her hand went round the eleven strips. "I have one floor. If a hook is cut a little wide on the ninth stone, I cannot tell whether that is a different kind, or the same kind and a tired arm. Nobody could, from here. So I shall count. And I shall not number the kinds from one site."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:75:"That's the string?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:77:"It's been on the string since the hundredth. I spanned it nine times yesterday with your hook, to learn it. I'd have asked first whether nine was too many, but you were asleep."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:79:"It isn't too many. It's hide by now." She let the hand go. "You've the hook on your belt. Keep it there. That bow's no good to anybody with its hook in my pannier, and I ought to have known it at noon on that hill."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:139:She had kept it for the doubtful ones, and there were a good many grains in it. A hook with two bars across the stem, and on another stone a hook with two bars set a hair closer. A long stroke crossed at a slant, and a long stroke crossed at a slant that leaned a little the other way. She had dropped each of those into the bowl, and gone on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:151:Eleven stones, on one floor, rubbed in one afternoon by one hand. Every difference on those strips was a difference that she could not weigh. A wide hook might be a word. It might be a mason whose arm was tired at the ninth stone, or a flaw in the grey, or her own pencil slipping while Lira shifted her palm. She could not ask the mason. She could not go back to the stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:153:So she had a count, and no number that she would say. The grains in the cup were the kinds that she would swear to. The grains in the bowl were the kinds that only a second floor could give her or take away. If there was a second floor, and the hook stood wide on it in the same place, then it was a kind, and it came out of the bowl. If it stood narrow, it had been an arm.

