editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:13:"Lira's had that since dusk. The rest of you have it now. It's fourteen days late, and I'm sorry for every one of them." He looked at the square woman by the ladder. "And there's one thing more that I have, from the night of the birthday, which none of you is getting. I read myself, as she set it. What I found is hers before it's anybody's. I'm telling you that it exists, so that nobody learns later that I sat on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:27:"I have a row to amend," she said. Her voice was whole. "*Stated by him, not tested by me* stands, since it is still true. I am adding beneath it: *tested by him, at noon on the 96th, unwitnessed; told on the 110th*. And now I want something that I have never had. I have had that hillside for fourteen nights in five heads, and not one account of it on paper. I want what each of you saw. In the order in which you saw it. Nothing that you supposed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:111:"You have been a sentence in somebody's box since you were fourteen. There is a word on a file in the west that was put there by a form, since the form could not be filled in, and you have had to walk about under it. I have watched what that cost." Her hand lay on the lid. "There is nothing in this box about you that you have not been told to your face, and there is not going to be. That is all that I kept you for."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:279:He had had the order of it for fifteen days. He had settled it on the evening that five people stopped at a rise and he did not: there was a thing that he would have to say, when he had it, and Lira was to hear it first, and after her the rest. He had not kept to that. He had told a healer the oldest part of it on a table, since that debt would not wait. But the part that was Lira's he had carried past a stone, and a rut, and ten nights in a loft; and she had let him carry it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:321:He had not known how much he had been waiting for somebody to say that it was enough. He had carried a half-made sentence for fifteen days as a thing to be ashamed of, since it had no end to it; and she had taken it out of his hands, and looked at it, and told him that a half was what there was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:65:*one dark since fourteen; no change either side of the line; stated by him, not tested by me*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:313:She shut her mouth on what she had been about to say, since it was true. She had been a Wind since she was fourteen, and she had never in her life sat down without some part of her already on the way to getting up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:241:She raised her eyes. The loft's boards were over her head, and the sacks, and four people asleep. One of them had gone up a hill for her with fire in his fist. He had turned leaves for her for three days and written what she called, and had not once asked her what she thought. She did not suppose that he had failed to wonder. He had been a sentence in other people's boxes since he was fourteen, and she had spent two years watching what that had cost him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:289:"It gives what was observed, and it does not go a step past the observing. I should have been glad of more words of that kind, in fifteen years." He considered the leaf. "There will be another name for this in the west, on the docket of a tin. I never read the docket. I do not know what they call it, and I shall not guess."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:131:The hold had eaten. Its fourteen had drawn off to the far end of that low room, to their mending and their dice and an old man asleep in the chimney corner, and they did not stare; but Cael felt himself counted from under a good many eyelids. The board had been scrubbed. Six bowls stood on it, and six horn spoons, and a round of bread that was black on both faces.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:387:Below him the yard was white and empty. On the platform by the western gate a lean man stood where he always stood. Cael shut the book on nineteen, and put it under his hand, and lay down beside a wall of other people's barley.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:5:"I was fourteen. It was a district office on a cold morning, with a chalk circle on the boards for me to stand in. Something woke behind my breastbone, as it wakes in everybody on that day: a small light at the edge of my eye. You'd call it an Arbiter." He kept his voice low and his breath slow, as she had taught it to go. "It looked at me for eleven seconds, and I counted them. There's a plate let into the desk in those offices, which lights with the name of your Path. For eleven seconds it stayed dark."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:175:"You've been a thing that nobody could read since you were fourteen. I've stood at that door five times and once more for twenty minutes, and I've a list to keep, and I came all the same." For the first time that morning he heard something in her voice that was not arithmetic. "I want to know what you find, and I want to be the one you tell."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:83:He lay and counted them, and there were nine. It was like a room he had lived in for eighteen years with the lights suddenly on. Every stick in it was where he had always known that it was. He had crossed that floor ten thousand times in the dark without barking a shin; and he had never once seen it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:129:He had done fourteen, and gone to the well to drink, when the heaviest thing in him moved.

