editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:27:"I have a row to amend," she said. Her voice was whole. "*Stated by him, not tested by me* stands, since it is still true. I am adding beneath it: *tested by him, at noon on the 96th, unwitnessed; told on the 110th*. And now I want something that I have never had. I have had that hillside for fourteen nights in five heads, and not one account of it on paper. I want what each of you saw. In the order in which you saw it. Nothing that you supposed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:223:Oryn went out by the northern wicket as the hundred and eleventh day was breaking, and for the first quarter of a mile she kept putting her hand out for a cheek-strap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:245:Out of that gate on the hundred and eleventh, when she had said the ninety-fifth: sixteen. Two days of road to the next. Two gates after it. Then the ford with the oxen, where they had last seen her on the eighty-fourth and had looked for her on the hundred and fourth; she would come down to it on the hundred and twenty-first, if nothing broke, and that was seventeen.
