editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:205:The third finding was a thing to be looked at, and Karis laid it out on the trestle on the hundred and seventh morning like a stall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:283:On the hundred and eighth day there was no rail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:3:The fourth finding was not about stone, and Karis gave it on the hundred and eighth morning in a different voice from the others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:59:It was the evening of the hundred and ninth day, with the light gone from the shutter and the hold's lantern standing on the trestle among the chips. She had asked the four of them to stay down after the day's slips were counted; and when they had settled, on kegs and sacks and the foot of the ladder, she let a good while go by before she spoke. She had three leaves laid side by side on the scrubbed board, and a fourth beside them, which was the rubbing of the founding stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:125:The healer read the arm at first light on the hundred and tenth day, in the yard, with the frost still white on the well.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:161:"You I can tell nothing. You'd know before I would." She rubbed her palms together. "I go in the morning. That's sixteen out of this gate. A round never gives a day back; it takes one. So I'll call it seventeen at Oxhollow, where there's a table that's been looking for me since the hundred and fourth."
