editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:3:Karis opened the middle box on the hundred and third morning, under a roof, as she had said at a stone that she would.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:65:The healer came to the barn door at noon on the hundred and fourth day, and she stopped there at her stride and a half, as she stopped everywhere.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:99:"I gave a day for this gate, the last time that I gave any, and by that I'd have been out of it on the ninety-fifth. That's nine late here." A thick finger folded down. "There are two days more on that bench before I've paid what I owe it, the way the carts are coming. So I go on the hundred and sixth, and that's eleven late at the next."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:125:On the night of the hundred and third day, when the others had gone up the ladder, Karis stayed on her keg in the middle of the barn with the hold's lantern and a horn cup.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:155:She drew a line under the figure. *Counted on the 103rd. One site. Not to be given out as the number of kinds.* And beneath that, since she kept that account too: *I want very much to give it out.*
