editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:17:Lira pinned it. She could do that much with one hand: she had a bag of Brom's river pebbles hung at her belt, and she went down on her knees and set one on each corner of the strip without letting the left arm so much as stir in its sling.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:21:It took an hour. When it was done, the strips lay on the clay like the spokes of a wheel, eleven of them, each in the place of its own stone, with their heads toward the keg and their feet toward the walls. Beside every one stood a peeled wand, cut by Seln to her measure and set upright in a lump of well-clay. They had used a thumb's length for a foot. The tallest wand stood as high as Brom's shin, and the shortest was hardly more than a peg.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:37:"I do not know that these are letters. I said so on the floor, and nothing has altered. But somebody cut them one stroke after another, with gaps between, and a thing that is cut in groups can be counted in groups." She laid her finger under a hooked stroke with two short bars across its stem. "That. I want to know whether that is on any other strip. If it is on four, it is a kind. If it is on this one alone, it is a scratch, and I shall say so."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:67:Cael had been watching her from the trestle for two mornings, in the breaths between slips. The bench under her window was never empty now. The carts from the upper holds had begun to come down for their barley, a cart or two each day, and every one of them brought somebody who had been saving a hurt for the healer's round: a hand, a back, a child who would not eat. She went along them at first light and again when the shadow reached the well. He had counted five mendings in two days and a good many more that were only looking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:69:She had not spoken to him since the yard. She had sat at a board with him, and had eaten a goat; it was not the same thing, and he did not make the mistake of taking it for one. A person may sit at a table with somebody whom she has not let off, as she may mend a man whom she does not like.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:91:She put no question about the lines. She had seen a table with somebody else's work on it, and had given it the one look that a person gives to a trade which is not hers; and Cael, who had been read for most of his life by people who could not leave a strange thing alone, thought that he had never seen it done so cleanly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:121:It was not kindly said, and it was kindly meant. Lira laid the tenth strip down, and set her four pebbles on it one by one, and got up off the clay on her flat foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:133:It was more than she had expected, and that was the first thing. She had sat at lectures on old hands. She owned copies of nine alphabets, in the left-hand box, three of them out of a restricted room; and the largest had fewer signs than she had grains. If these were letters, it was a prodigal sort of language. If they were not letters, then she did not know what a person cuts into stone, group after group, with gaps between.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:135:She did not know. She made herself say it over, there on the keg. It was the truest thing on the floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:151:Eleven stones, on one floor, rubbed in one afternoon by one hand. Every difference on those strips was a difference that she could not weigh. A wide hook might be a word. It might be a mason whose arm was tired at the ninth stone, or a flaw in the grey, or her own pencil slipping while Lira shifted her palm. She could not ask the mason. She could not go back to the stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:153:So she had a count, and no number that she would say. The grains in the cup were the kinds that she would swear to. The grains in the bowl were the kinds that only a second floor could give her or take away. If there was a second floor, and the hook stood wide on it in the same place, then it was a kind, and it came out of the bowl. If it stood narrow, it had been an arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:159:There was a boy asleep over her head who now carried a thing that could read how a person was built. She had entered that she knew it was not hers. She looked at eleven strips of paper in a lantern's light, and thought that an instrument was a fine thing to have, and that paper was what she had been given.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:173:They were a carter's words, though, and a carter had never been in. He meant a patch of country that his road went round. *Ground* was a where.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:191:There was *silencing*, which she held longest. It was the right sort of word; it was made out of a verb, and a verb was what she had seen. But it claimed too much. To silence a thing was to stop its mouth, and she had copied a great many pages on what an Arbiter was, in a room where the pages were counted before she left it, and not one of them said that it spoke.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:199:It was made out of a verb, like the other; it named a thing that went on happening, to whatever came over the line, for as long as it stayed there. It did not say how. It did not say why. It took *the* in front, which made it one thing, and it would take a place after it without complaint: the Quieting at the Fallow Ring. She could write *inside* before it, and *the edge of*, and *a site of*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:211:It had come in her sleep, whole, as the answer to a long sum sometimes comes when a person has stopped doing it. She lay between two sacks of barley with her eyes open and looked at it. Then she got up, very quietly, and put Seln's coat on over her shirt, and went down the ladder with the lantern unlit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:221:The third was the perimeter, sixty-two stations, and against every one of them the same offset, in the same hand, sixty-two times.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:223:She had made each of those leaves by itself, on a different day, for a different purpose; and she had kept them in the box apart. She had never before set the three in a row.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:233:She had struck better sentences than this one out of other people's notes. She had done it kindly, as a rule, with a single line through the middle, and a word in the margin about the difference between what a person has seen and what a person has supposed; and she had never once thought, while she did it, what it was to be the one holding the pen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:235:She made herself look at the fear, as she had looked at her own tidy rows at a stone on the night that she ruled them. She had been frightened then by how well a thing agreed, and she had entered the fright. This was not the same. Then she had been afraid that she was fooling herself. Now she was afraid that she was not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:237:It was not a finding. She held to that, with the pen in her hand and the cold of the barn coming up through her boots. Nothing on the three leaves said it. They only lay in a row, and let her say it for them; and a thing that the evidence merely permits is the most dangerous thing that a researcher ever has on her table, since nobody but herself will stop her from writing it down. If she wrote it down, it would be in her hand, in ink, in a box that she meant to outlive her; and whoever opened that box in a hundred years would take it for a thing that she had known.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:265:"It is a thing that happens. It happens to an Arbiter, at an edge, and it goes on happening for as long as that Arbiter is inside the edge; and then it stops, and nothing has been lost. Yours were the best words for that. I have built on them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:269:"The Quieting." She made a face. "It sounds like what you do to a baby."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:299:Cael looked at two words on a leaf. He knew, as well as anybody living, what a name could do to a thing. A clerk had once put one on a boy, in capitals, where a tier should have gone; and from that morning nobody had needed to ask what the boy was, since there was a word on a file that answered for him. A name of that sort shut a door. This was the first that he had ever watched being made which had been cut to keep the door open: it said what five people had seen, and stopped, and left the rest of the question standing where anybody might walk up to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:303:Out in the yard somebody was walking toward the western gate, flat-footed, with one arm tied.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:3:Karis read out her second finding on the hundred and sixth morning, and she began it with a table that all of them had seen made.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:37:"I was frightened at the stone, on the night that I ruled them, and I entered that I was. I have had eleven days to find out why." She put the pen down. "I sat at lectures on what an Arbiter is, and I copied a good many pages in a room where they counted the pages before I left. On one point every page agrees. It is a person's own. It wakes for one person, on one morning, and names one gift; mine is not Lira's, and Brom's is no kin to a healer's, and not one of them has anything to do with another."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:41:"A personal spiritual entity should not fail the way five looms fail when somebody shuts the sluice on the wheel that drives them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:57:He let that lie while a person might have drawn one breath, and his mild eyes stayed on the leaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:105:"It doesn't want hands. It's knitting, and it wants time, and letting alone. It wants somebody by who'd know the day it turned, if it means to turn, since nobody else in this hold could tell that from a bruise." She pulled the wool up again and tied her knot. "A mend that deep is all one thing until it's old. If a corner of it goes on a road, with nobody there to see, she's lost the hand by the snow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:121:"I know who I've got on that road a good deal better than you do." She said it without heat. "And I'll not have spent a night of myself on an arm and then walked off from it in the week that it wanted watching. Those are my five days. You didn't ask for them, and you're not to carry them. Go and shoot your bow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:131:She had gone up the ladder behind the lean man with her right hand and both knees, and had stood up on the planks with the whole valley in front of her. There was the stretch, a mile of pale frozen grass between two shoulders of hill. There was the white stone, a hundred and ten yards down it. And forty paces out from the gate somebody had driven a stake, and hung a sack on it that was stuffed with straw, with a ring burnt on the sacking as big as a plate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:135:It was a bow, and it was not his kind. His own leaned against the fence, as tall as he was. This was short and thick, an ash stock as long as her arm with a bow of horn and wood lashed across the nose of it, and an iron stirrup, and a long lever underneath. It was very old. Somebody had kept it oiled.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:139:She looked at it, and she did what she had made up her mind on the ladder to do, which was to say the thing before he could find it out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:149:So that was how it would be. She would never loose a bolt that somebody else had not bent his back for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:151:"A bow wants one hand," said the bowman, "and a thing that doesn't move."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:177:She had stood in a yard a quarter of an hour before and watched a healer unload a mule. That made five days; and there was a child at some gate with a chest that wanted listening to, who would wait those five days longer, since a girl had run along a rim on a leg that she had been told not to run on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:187:She took her face off the wood. She had not made a sound. She had not declared anything since a ledge on a mountain, and he had stood on that walk for a week and watched her not do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:191:She sat down on the planks with her back against a post. He did not explain. He stood with his own long bow at the length of his arm and looked down the valley; and by and by she understood that he meant to stand there until she was finished, however long that took.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:209:"Oldest to the right," she said. "This first is a district hall's, and I had it off the step myself; it was dressed in my grandmother's time. The pale one is from a library that I used to be allowed into, and the honey-coloured is Ostrand's wall. And that is the oldest dressed stone between Denvash and the Line that anybody can put a year to. A mason was re-laying a drain at the foot of the old gate, the one that the city counts its years from. I call it the founding stone, and I have never owned anything older."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:217:"That is the tool. A stone that has been dressed has been struck a great many times by a person with a chisel, and the strokes stay." She did the same to the second and the third, and on each the lines were wider apart, and softer. On the founding stone they were so faint that Lira had to carry the paper to the door. "They wear, but they are there. I have never rubbed a dressed stone and found none."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:221:"The band is cut into it, and the band came up. Now look at the ground that the band is cut in." The paper there was one even grey from edge to edge. "I rubbed eleven stones a hand above that floor, and there is not a tool-mark on one of them, except the lines that somebody meant. I entered *no stroke* on the afternoon, and did not know what I had."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:251:"So I cannot tell you how it was made, since I have nothing to compare it to. But a thing may be dated without knowing how it was made, if it has stood in the weather, and that has." She took up the cup of wands. "I wrote down which way every stone faced. I did it from habit, and it is the best thing that I did on that floor."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:255:"It is the same band, cut by the same hand to the same depth; a fingernail, he said, and I measured it." On the southern strip the lines stood sharp and white. On the northern they were grey and narrow, and in two places the pencil had not found them at all. "That is weather. That is a north wind on the Fallow, taking a little off a stone in every cold term. Now the founding stone." She put its rubbing between the two strips. "It has stood in a north wall for as long as that city has had a gate. Look at how much of the tool is left on it, and then look at how much of that band is left on the north."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:267:She rested her throat. Cael saw that she was choosing how to say the rest, and he saw also that she had made up her mind to say all of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:315:She thought of an oak floor on a bluff, and of a man at a rail who had made her walk a count for a month: three lies and a truth, with a step on the fourth where a burst would have been. He had struck her for bursting. But there had always been the Third-day at the end of it, when he let them off the count, and a person could go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:329:"That's not a reason to teach somebody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:331:He thought about it. She had learned by now that he could not be hurried, and that he could not be made to say a word more than a thing was worth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:17:"She said that I'd turned her table round. Nobody lies down on it without being told what she means to do to them, and this morning the one who wasn't told was the healer." He made himself give them the rest of it as she had given it to him. "She said the part that she minded most was the four of you. I hadn't done it without knowing its name. I'd been told the name by four people, and agreed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:19:Nobody moved on the sacks, and Brom was looking at his own boots. Cael had thought that this would be the hardest sentence to carry up a ladder, and it was. Four people had done the one thing that friends can do for somebody who means to go wrong, which is to say so to his face; and a healer had counted it against him, rightly, that they had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:47:He understood her, and he saw that the others did. She had spent two years ruling a leaf about what he was, from the outside, with a curve on it and a column, and she had wanted the result of that morning more than she had ever wanted a result; she had written so against herself in the dark. Now there was a thing in him that read how people were built, and she would never ask him to turn it on a wall, or on a stranger, or on herself. She had put that down, in the only voice that she had, before she could so much as begin to wish it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:75:Compression made no sound of its own. He found it as he would have found a rope in a dark barn, by leaning on it: the whole of his frame creaked, very faintly, like hemp under a load.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:125:So Brom took two sacks at a time, one on each shoulder, and went up the rungs like a man going upstairs to bed. Seln took one, and made no sound with it. Karis sat on an upturned keg at the ladder's foot with the hold's slate on her knee, and cut a stroke for every sack that passed her. Lira had been given nothing, since there was nothing that a person could do with a sack and one arm. She sat on the well's coping with her foot flat on the stones and counted the strokes on Karis's slate, aloud, to annoy her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:131:He had his left hand on the stone rim and the dipper in his right. He heard it begin, and that was new; it had shifted in him before, on a bluff and on a road and in a loft two nights ago, and each time he had known of it only as a ship's passenger knows that the cargo has slid. Now he heard the whole of it. The low sound rose, and went over from one side of him to the other in a long slow roll, as a sea goes over a bar, and it took as long about it as a person takes to draw a bucket. Then it lay down again, and where it lay was a little lower than before, and a little to one side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:133:It had made room. There was a new thing under his breastbone, as light as a wren, and the heaviest of the ten had moved over for it as a great dog moves over on a hearth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:145:He had laid no lane, and so this was not a bill. The thing had moved house, and it meant to be late until it had done unpacking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:161:He knew what she meant. It had moved twice in one week on a bluff, for a quarter of an hour and then for forty minutes; and nobody had tried to hit him during either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:169:It was her drill, or it was the drill that an instructor had built for her on a bluff: three lies and a truth. The front foot went first, and then the back, and then the shoulders; and on the fourth count there was a step where a burst would have been. He had walked it beside her for two years.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:199:He tried that, twice. It was harder than it sounded, since he had loaded it on the three for as long as he had owned it, and his shoulders did the thing before he could forbid them. He held it off with his jaw tight and asked on the four; and each time the lean came after his foot was already planted, and he rocked on the spot like a post that somebody has kicked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:227:He stood there for a breath with his mouth open. It had cost half of what it had ever cost him; and for once he had no need to reckon that afterward from an ache, as he reckoned every other price that he paid, since he had heard it while it was being spent, as plainly as a person hears the difference between a door slammed and a door shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:231:He did it again. *Four, and.* He did it four times more after that, there and back between the well and the trough, with the lean coming up short and clean on the half-beat and lying down the moment that it was done; and in none of them did he go a step that he had not meant.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:245:*Hundred and first day. The yard, at noon, inside the fence. Wind, eleven leans of a foot each, to learn it with the ground late. That is practice, and I am not putting it in the number; the number is what I spend on a road, or in a fight, or on a person. I enter it here so that nobody has to take my word for what's left out.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:283:"I'll say the other half here, where there's sky over it. One day you'll want to put that hand on somebody else. On her, as like as not, or on the big one, to see what you find." Her voice had gone lower. "And when it turns outward, you ask. Every time. I ask the dying."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:285:He had known since a loft what it was to reach for somebody who had not been asked. He had done it the morning before, on her own table, with his eyes open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:21:"Somebody had to give one. I was the only person there who knew that it was a different ruling for me." He made himself go on. "I spent a quarter of an hour that I'd said I wouldn't, and I let six people stand at a den believing that all six were alike. If I had told you at the stone, at noon, you'd have planned for it. We'd have been in that lane by two hands, and Lira would have two arms."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:73:Cael waited for one of them to join those things together. He had sat in a good many rooms where it would have been done before the lamp was trimmed: a seat would have found a word for it, and a clerk would have written the word down, and by morning there would have been a file. Here six people sat round one lantern with a made floor, and an age that had no ruler, and a boy; and each of them left the three lying apart, as Karis had left them on a trestle, since nobody in that loft knew what lay between them and nobody was willing to pretend.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:95:"That's how you find a handful. One at a time, for money." She nodded toward Hesk's satchel on its nail, where a broker's cream paper lay folded in a book. "Somebody paid a hundred marks to learn whether a crew would come out of the first, and I do not think that he has stopped wanting to know."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:103:The healer had got to her feet. She had sat through the whole of it on the edge of a sack, with her hands on her knees, and Cael could not have said what she made of a calfskin with rings on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:109:"Don't go in without me. Not because I'm any use inside. Because somebody should be outside the line who can put an arm back on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:135:Cael stood at the loft door for some time afterward, with the frost coming in round his feet and the yard white below him. A boat had gone west on the hundred and second day with a clerk's fair copy in it, and that was eight days ago. He supposed that by now it lay on somebody's desk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:201:"Asking's the least of it, and it's not the whole. You put your hand on a person when there's something to read *for*. I've never yet laid a finger on anybody to see whether my fingers worked."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:219:Behind him somebody was coming down the ladder, heavily, with a box.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:225:It was not there, and she had known that it would not be. She had walked that track thirty times with a small brown head at her right shoulder, and her hand had thirty rounds of habit in it; it went out at every turn of the path, and closed on air, and came back. She let it. A person who has set a great many bones does not expect a habit to knit in a morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:257:She had told the big one that a person should not name a beast.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:259:She had never named that one. At the wicket she had learned that this made not the least difference.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:271:There was a piece of her craft in him, all the same. She knew that as she knew a mend of her own on somebody else's leg. She had felt it go along beside her and then go on alone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:281:She had told a loft that she was no use on the far side of that stone, and it was true. But she had also learned at that stone how long an arm keeps, bound, and how far a person can be carried. It came to about two hundred yards.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:283:So she would be at the edge. There would be another of those places; the small one had as good as promised it. Somebody ought to stand on the right side of the stone with her sleeves pushed up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:287:He had tried to pay her at the well. She had seen it coming across the yard, in the way that he carried the book; he was a boy who could not bear to owe, and he had wanted to be rid of the debt before she went, as a person wants a splinter out, without much caring how it is drawn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:291:He would read himself twice more. He was frightened of what he had found, and he would do it regardless, since he had been told to. Then he would sit on some bench of hers and wait his turn, and say it; and she would be the first living person to hear what the thing that she read with had made of the one body that it could not read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:301:It was a slow heavy knocking, a long way down the valley, with water falling under it. She knew that sound. There would be a wheel at the end of it, and a fence, and a keeper who counted people off a ridge. Beyond the fence there would be a bench, with whoever had sat down on it sixteen mornings ago when her mule should have come in sight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:23:"Six," she said, as a person says the weight of a sack that she has lifted before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:35:She did it six times, without dropping one and without a glance downward, as a person does a thing that she has learned in the dark. When the sixth lay on the stone she hooked the cord round her little finger and drew it tight against her belt, and held the purse out behind her again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:97:Karis had come up on the other side. She looked into the basket for about as long as she would have looked at a line of somebody else's sums.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:119:The small man looked at Cael then, for the first time, up and down, as he might have looked at a tree that somebody else had planted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:121:"It's you that it's for, then. I'd have put more years on you than they tell me. It's the way you look at a thing, as if you meant to write down what it weighed." He nodded, and was talking again before he had turned to go. "The kid's hung since noon yesterday, she'd no call to make a favour of it, and you'll want that fire built up an hour before—"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:133:"I made that," said Brom. "Seln offered. I said that a day like this wanted the proper bread."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:135:The kid was on a spit over the fire, and the small man was turning it and talking to it. The fire had been built for them, as Lira had bargained, twice as high as that hearth usually carried, and the heat of it came across the board like a hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:147:She took the place nearest the door. She sat on the end of the bench with her boots drawn under her, as a person sits who may have to get up, and laid her two hands on the board where she could see them. Cael watched her do it. She ate at seven gates, round after round, whatever was put before her, and went out again in the morning; and she sat at this one like a woman who has sat at a great many tables and stayed at none of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:175:"In the city we sang it for whoever was oldest on the bench," said Oryn. "It was never a good sign for them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:181:They ate; and the kid was very good, and the bread was as it always was, and Cael ate his between a person who had named a wrong to his face in a loft and a person who had just been told that he was three of somebody. Nothing in him asked for anything. The listening went along at the edge of the talk, quick and slow, and for the first time since a trough he was glad of the company.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:185:"Somebody's to say it," she said. "I said it the last time, across a table, before anybody else could. I'm not giving that up to a man with a spit."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:203:"Here, at the board, with us looking. You never write the good ones while they're happening. You write them afterward in a loft, by yourself, as if somebody might catch you at it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:213:For a breath only the spit made any sound, ticking as it turned. He had put four of them down as his own, without asking leave, and the fifth by the one name that she had ever given herself in their hearing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:221:It was then that somebody knocked the frost off his boots at the door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:257:He passed her the bark, and she read it for herself, once, and gave it to Karis. He had written a healer down as a stop, in pencil, a minute before a one-armed man's line came in at the door to say that the country had already entered her otherwise; and a clerk's fair copy of that same count was in a boat by now, going west to whoever had put the question.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:287:Karis bit it, and chewed for some time, with the look of a person reading a hard page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:321:The others slept, and he had waited until Brom's breathing went over into the long pull that meant an ox could have walked across him, and until the lantern had been dark for an hour. Then he lay down on his back on the boards, between a wall of barley and the ladder's head, with his boots off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:357:That made three things, and he had no fourth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:371:He had written the same three things under it at every inventory, on a bluff and a road and a shelf above a valley with no name, and they had been true each time. They had also been easy, since a person can be patient for a long while with a thing that he cannot see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:9:"Then it lit, late, with one word on it, and the word wasn't a Path. Karis found out what it is. It's what the form writes when it can't be filled in, and it stands where an older name used to be written, before somebody took the older name away. After that the light went out. It has been dark ever since, on a floor or a road or at your stone. When the five of you crossed that line, something stopped in each of you; and nothing changed in me, since nothing was lit to begin with."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:19:"What came after is the part that concerns you. When somebody with a Path uses it on me, or against me, for long enough, and means it, and there's something that matters to both of us in how it comes out, then a piece of what they do stays in me afterward. They still have all of theirs; I've looked, every time. But I have a piece as well. It's smaller than theirs, and it costs me to carry, and it answers when I ask."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:37:"This morning there's a tenth." He kept his eyes on her face, which hung over him upside down and grey. "It wants a real thing done to me for a long time, at a cost, by somebody who means it, and you had told me at Oxhollow what the long reading was: both hands, and all of you, for minutes. I knew when I lay down what it was likely to be. At the end there was a place in me standing open, and the thing that you read with was at the door. I could have kept that door shut. I'm better at shutting them than at anything else I do. I opened it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:41:"When one comes, there are lines. I see them, and nobody else can; they've come every time, in the same place, like a notice pinned to a board." He said them over to her as they stood, six lines from the top, leaving nothing out: what it was adjacent to, and that it was partial, and its tier, and the two things that it was made of, and its range, which was the length of an arm and no farther. He gave her the last line more slowly than the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:59:"There's a leaf," said Cael. "I wrote it last night in the loft, before I had so much as crossed your yard. Everything that I knew is on it, and what I had made up my mind to do. You ought to read it before you answer, since it's worse than what I've told you and it's truer."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:71:The grey of the yard came in, and the cold with it. Brom was sitting on the bench under the window, alone, with his hands on his knees. He had come down the ladder before it was light, Cael understood, and had put himself where a person could be sent from; he was on his feet before she had opened her mouth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:83:"Shut it. Now open it." He did both, and four dark lines went across the pads of four fingers and came back. "It'll draw a string. Did you know what he meant to do on my table?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:147:"I meant—"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:149:"I know what you meant. You meant to be square. You'd take whatever I gave you and call the account shut." She shook her head, once. "You don't set it. That's the first of what it costs. You're not the one who knows the price, and you'll go about not knowing it until I do, which won't be today."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:191:She did not keep one. She had told the small pale one so across a fire: a healer who wanted a page to know who was owed had stopped looking at them. It was the truest thing that she knew about her trade, and here she sat on her own stool, in her own room, with somebody else's page open on her knee and her thumb in the margin of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:201:She shut the book and laid her hands on its cover, palms down, and went over what they had found. She did it as her teacher had made her do it, for a second healer who was not in the room, with her knuckles ready to be struck for a guess.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:203:There had been skin, and breath, and a heart that somebody had run hard since it was small. Past those there had been the moving, more than one, with no bank anywhere; she had looked for twenty minutes and she would have looked for forty. That much she had said aloud while she was in, since it was truer said so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:207:She had not said that part aloud. She had taken it for tiredness. In a long reading the healer goes along the patient and comes home to her own left hand, over and over, and toward the end she begins to hear herself as a person hears her own boots on a road at night. This had been like hearing a second pair. They had fallen exactly with hers, with the same short step on the right, where her heel was raw.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:227:She had known it by the fourth line of his leaf, as she had told him, and she did not say it over now, even to herself, since a thing said over in an empty room is half given away. She turned the keeping of it over once instead, to be certain that she had her reasons straight and was not merely being hard. If the answer had been yes, then to tell him so was a pardon. If it had been no, it was a sentence, and a person can serve a sentence and be done. Either way he would have had from her, a day late, the very thing that he had gone round her to avoid, which was an end to not knowing. He had made her answer into a thing that never happened. It could stay so, and he could do his own carrying.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:249:That made it no better, and she had told him that it would not. She was glad, though, in the plain way that a person is glad of her tools, and she rubbed her hands together for some time after the bench was empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:267:She went back to her room. She had a bench to pay that was owed five days now, and a round behind it that was owed a good many more; and a boy in that hold had a piece of her craft in him that he did not know how to use. She was hungry to hear what it would find, and she did not take that for forgiveness. She had been hungry to read him for sixteen days, and it had not made her fond of his ribs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:277:He ate it standing, and tasted none of it. Nobody came to him. Brom looked across once from the bench under the window, and got up, and climbed the ladder to the loft. On the platform by the western gate a lean man was watching the valley, as he did at every hour of every day. Cael had been left alone with a bowl by four people who knew when a person was not fit to be spoken to; and when the bowl was empty he went to break the ice for the geese, since it wanted doing, and since it could be done by somebody who was not thinking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:293:This one asked for nothing. It had sat down in him like a person at a window, and it was attending. There was no hand held out for him to put aside. He could no more have refused it than he could have refused his own ears.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:9:"Because a thing that is written twice is a thing that somebody means to use," said Seln. "I have filed a great deal of paper. I know which leaves are drawn out of a box first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:13:"One. A bar of iron of the registry's pattern, bearing its stamp and the number thirty-one, stands on the edge of the Quieting at the Fallow Ring, a boot's width from the place where I found that edge with my feet. Two. By its rust, and by the turf grown against it, it has stood there about five years. Three. It was driven at the far end of a measure, so that whoever drove it had found the same circle that I paced, and had a cord or a chain to find it with. Four. I do not know what the number counts, or what was written down afterward, or where it is kept."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:15:She lowered the leaf. It was the shortest thing that she had read out in six days, and she had plainly cut it down from something a good deal longer; there was not an *as though* in it, or a *we suppose*, and every noun in the four sentences was a thing that somebody in that barn had stood over.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:47:"There is a thing that I should add, and it is not for the box," he said. "You have put *I do not know where it is kept*. That is exact. I should only like it remembered in this barn that it is kept; and that six days ago a boat went west, to tell whoever keeps it that a crew walked into that ring and out again."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:53:Karis laid the roll in the middle box, by itself, at one end, a little apart from the name and the five rows and the founding stone, as a person sets down on a bench the one tool that belongs to somebody else's trade.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:87:He had one of his own, which had come to him at a camp by a stone, with an arm two hours old lying beside him, and he had written in Hesk's book that he would not invent a word for it. He had not gone back to that sentence since. He had only added to what stood under it: a tenth thing, and a place in the middle of himself that he was not yet free to speak of.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:91:The others went up; and Brom took the lantern's fellow from its nail and lit Lira up the ladder ahead of him, a rung at a time, with his big hand held ready under her heel and never touching it. He had been told what that arm might not do, and had decided for himself what the rest of her was likely to want. Seln went last and made no sound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:99:"The circle's perfect," she said. She was looking at the shut box. "Perfect isn't natural. Somebody made a floor that turns Paths off. It's older than anything I can date. And it doesn't turn you off. Three facts. I'm not putting a verb between them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:111:"You have been a sentence in somebody's box since you were fourteen. There is a word on a file in the west that was put there by a form, since the form could not be filled in, and you have had to walk about under it. I have watched what that cost." Her hand lay on the lid. "There is nothing in this box about you that you have not been told to your face, and there is not going to be. That is all that I kept you for."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:321:He had not known how much he had been waiting for somebody to say that it was enough. He had carried a half-made sentence for fifteen days as a thing to be ashamed of, since it had no end to it; and she had taken it out of his hands, and looked at it, and told him that a half was what there was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:337:She made a small sound that was nearly a laugh.

