editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:3:Karis read out her second finding on the hundred and sixth morning, and she began it with a table that all of them had seen made.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:37:"I was frightened at the stone, on the night that I ruled them, and I entered that I was. I have had eleven days to find out why." She put the pen down. "I sat at lectures on what an Arbiter is, and I copied a good many pages in a room where they counted the pages before I left. On one point every page agrees. It is a person's own. It wakes for one person, on one morning, and names one gift; mine is not Lira's, and Brom's is no kin to a healer's, and not one of them has anything to do with another."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:113:"I'll read it at the fortnight," she said, over her shoulder. "That's the hundred and tenth. If it's taken by then, it's taken."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:115:"You said the hundred and sixth." Lira's voice cracked in the middle. "You said eleven at the next."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:129:It had begun for Lira two days earlier, on the hundred and fourth, at noon, on the gate-walk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:175:She came up on the hundred and sixth with her jaw shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:205:The third finding was a thing to be looked at, and Karis laid it out on the trestle on the hundred and seventh morning like a stall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:221:"The band is cut into it, and the band came up. Now look at the ground that the band is cut in." The paper there was one even grey from edge to edge. "I rubbed eleven stones a hand above that floor, and there is not a tool-mark on one of them, except the lines that somebody meant. I entered *no stroke* on the afternoon, and did not know what I had."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:229:"The third is the edge. A dressed stone is squared to sit on its neighbour, with a face and a bed and an arris between." She set the district hall's chip upright, on a side that was as true as a book's. "I went round every one of those eleven on my knees, and not one has a laid edge. They are not set on that floor. They come up out of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:237:"A knife marks a stone. On the afternoon of the ninety-sixth, while Lira was pinning the fourth sheet, I put this point to the outside of that rim, low down on the north, where a scratch would spoil nothing, and I leaned on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:253:She had Lira lift two strips to the light, side by side: the stone that had faced south-east, and the stone that had faced due north.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:255:"It is the same band, cut by the same hand to the same depth; a fingernail, he said, and I measured it." On the southern strip the lines stood sharp and white. On the northern they were grey and narrow, and in two places the pencil had not found them at all. "That is weather. That is a north wind on the Fallow, taking a little off a stone in every cold term. Now the founding stone." She put its rubbing between the two strips. "It has stood in a north wall for as long as that city has had a gate. Look at how much of the tool is left on it, and then look at how much of that band is left on the north."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:259:"The breaks are worse," said Karis. "Every one of those eleven is broken, and I looked at every break that I could reach. There is not a sharp place on one of them. They are as round as river stones, and nothing runs over the top of a pillar twice Brom's height but air."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:269:"It's older, I'll say once and not again, than [UNBOUND] — older than the word they retired, and I can't say by how much. Entered as a limit, not a finding. We explained the middle of the story in Ostrand. This is before the beginning, and I don't have a ruler for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:283:On the hundred and eighth day there was no rail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:31:"His face," said Lira. "On the floor, a foot from mine, over the dog's head. He was choosing. I didn't know between what. Then in the lane the grass leaned on my left hand with nobody in it. And then the air cracked, twenty feet north, and it was my crack."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:61:"One: it is a thing that happens, and I have called it the Quieting. Two: five sigils of five Paths failed at one stride, in one way, and came back so. Three: the floor is older than the oldest stone that I can date. Four: the registry has put iron on the edge of it." She shut the notebook on her finger. "And there is a hypothesis, which is mine, and which is not entered anywhere."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:81:"That one I have walked. I have its round, and its radius, and eleven sheets." The finger moved a long way west and south, to a blank among the carter's hill-hatching. "That is a day south of the ferry and a little aside; I rang it on the ninety-second evening, and wrote *guess*." It moved again, far down toward the corner of the hide, where the strokes of his water lay thick. "That is a long way south and east, with no road inside a day of it, and it is a guess as well. And the fourth is not on this skin at all." She lifted her eyes to the loft door. "It is on a sheet that he counted once, as a boy, and cannot place. One sheet had two."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:135:Cael stood at the loft door for some time afterward, with the frost coming in round his feet and the yard white below him. A boat had gone west on the hundred and second day with a clerk's fair copy in it, and that was eight days ago. He supposed that by now it lay on somebody's desk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:167:"You said that you'd say when, and you go in an hour. I read myself on the night of the hundred and second, as you set it, alone and on my back. I found—"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:213:She slung the pannier on her back and went to the mule, and laid her hand once on the brown neck. Then she walked away across the yard toward the northern fence, on the low side of nothing, and she went through the wicket without a glance behind her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:217:*Nothing out here reads me. And at a line you can mark with a rock, every Path in this camp stops, and mine doesn't. Karis won't write her hypothesis. I won't write mine. We're going to find the other circles. Pike said nobody goes in twice. Tomorrow makes three.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:223:Oryn went out by the northern wicket as the hundred and eleventh day was breaking, and for the first quarter of a mile she kept putting her hand out for a cheek-strap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:239:The track went up through the hold's grazing, past a stone hut, toward a low place in the hill. The goats lifted their heads to see her go by with nothing on a rope. She had a pannier on her own back, with the strap across her chest, and it rode where no pack had ridden since her first year: tape, flask, shears, linen, a cup, a needle, and a loaf. She had the spare rope over her shoulder. It was thirty feet of good three-strand, and she had laid it up herself; and she had carried it for eleven rounds without once needing it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:245:Out of that gate on the hundred and eleventh, when she had said the ninety-fifth: sixteen. Two days of road to the next. Two gates after it. Then the ford with the oxen, where they had last seen her on the eighty-fourth and had looked for her on the hundred and fourth; she would come down to it on the hundred and twenty-first, if nothing broke, and that was seventeen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:249:She had listened to that chest on the eighty-ninth evening, before she ate, and it had been no worse. They would have looked for her there on the hundred and ninth. That was two days ago already. She would not hear it again until the hundred and twenty-sixth at the soonest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:269:They were as they had been. She had mended eleven people at that gate since a morning on a plank, and every one of them had come up under her palms with banks and a bed. She had laid them on a girl's arm a fortnight old and felt eight places take. Nothing in them was missing. She had told the boy so, since he had a right to know what he had not broken.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:297:It had gone north from the wicket, and over the low place, and north again. At the first water it turned along the bank, as a carter's road will, and by the middle of the day she had the sun on her left cheek, and by the afternoon it was in her eyes. The water grew. Another came into it, and a third. There were alders, and then a hedge, and a place where wheels had been in and out of the stream for a great many years.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:21:"I'd priced it for her on the leaf. Whatever it cost me, down to her going north without me. She won't have that. She says a patient doesn't hand her his own fine ready counted, and that I'm not the one who knows the price, and shan't be until she does." He drew a breath. "And she knows what she'd have said last night, if I had knocked. She's keeping it. I'm not to be told, today or ever."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:55:"It's the hundredth," he said, and held his hand out to Karis, palm upward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:119:The grain came on the hundred and first morning, in three carts from the river country, and by the middle of the forenoon it was going up the ladder past the place where they slept.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:151:She had left off counting sacks. She was watching him come away from the well, a stride at a time, with the look that she had worn at the rail of a practice floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:167:He had ten strides of frozen yard between the well and the trough, and he had walked them in elevens with a bitten leg. He went to the well's end and set his feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:171:He had not asked that thing for anything since a rut on a hill, and not once in a hundred days for practice. But this was a yard, at noon, inside a fence, where a healer mended with her door open and a researcher might light a lamp; and if he was to be half a beat behind himself on some road next week, he would sooner learn what that was like here, among geese.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:241:On the platform by the western gate the bowman had turned round. It was the one hour of the day when nothing had ever come up that valley, and he seemed to keep it for looking inward, at the hold. He had watched a boy fall about a yard eleven times, and get it right, and he stood with his bow at the length of his arm and gave no sign that it was any business of his. Then his eyes went to the girl on the coping, who had seen a fault from thirty feet with one arm tied, and they stayed there a good deal longer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:245:*Hundred and first day. The yard, at noon, inside the fence. Wind, eleven leans of a foot each, to learn it with the ground late. That is practice, and I am not putting it in the number; the number is what I spend on a road, or in a fight, or on a person. I enter it here so that nobody has to take my word for what's left out.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:291:He carried eleven sacks more before the light went, on feet that told him of each rung a little after he had climbed it, and he did not fall off the ladder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:3:Karis opened the middle box on the hundred and third morning, under a roof, as she had said at a stone that she would.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:5:She had waited seven days to do it. Cael had watched her carry that restraint across the Fallow and up a ladder and through a birthday, with eleven sheets lying dry in the dark not six feet from her blanket; and he had understood that it was a kind of manners. She would not look at a thing until she could look at all of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:7:So she had the barn floor swept first. It was a threshing floor of beaten clay, twenty feet by thirty, and Brom went over it twice with a besom while the hold's hens stood by and resented him. She had the sack-menders' trestle carried to the north wall, under the shutter, for the chips and the brass and her three boxes. Then she had a keg set in the middle of the swept clay, and sat down on it, and held out her hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:15:"West by north. Lay it there. The foot of it outward."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:21:It took an hour. When it was done, the strips lay on the clay like the spokes of a wheel, eleven of them, each in the place of its own stone, with their heads toward the keg and their feet toward the walls. Beside every one stood a peeled wand, cut by Seln to her measure and set upright in a lump of well-clay. They had used a thumb's length for a foot. The tallest wand stood as high as Brom's shin, and the shortest was hardly more than a peg.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:55:"I was wrong about apples." Karis rested her throat, and went on. "I looked into one basket, on one day, and I said *two*. A man who had kept those trees for twenty-six years showed me three. This is one basket." Her hand went round the eleven strips. "I have one floor. If a hook is cut a little wide on the ninth stone, I cannot tell whether that is a different kind, or the same kind and a tired arm. Nobody could, from here. So I shall count. And I shall not number the kinds from one site."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:65:The healer came to the barn door at noon on the hundred and fourth day, and she stopped there at her stride and a half, as she stopped everywhere.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:77:"It's been on the string since the hundredth. I spanned it nine times yesterday with your hook, to learn it. I'd have asked first whether nine was too many, but you were asleep."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:83:It was the only time that he saw her look at it. She took in the swept clay, and the eleven pale strips lying like spokes, and the wands in their clay, and a small person on a keg in the middle with barley at her feet. Her look went round them once. It took about as long as it took her to count a bench.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:99:"I gave a day for this gate, the last time that I gave any, and by that I'd have been out of it on the ninety-fifth. That's nine late here." A thick finger folded down. "There are two days more on that bench before I've paid what I owe it, the way the carts are coming. So I go on the hundred and sixth, and that's eleven late at the next."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:125:On the night of the hundred and third day, when the others had gone up the ladder, Karis stayed on her keg in the middle of the barn with the hold's lantern and a horn cup.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:159:There was a boy asleep over her head who now carried a thing that could read how a person was built. She had entered that she knew it was not hers. She looked at eleven strips of paper in a lantern's light, and thought that an instrument was a fine thing to have, and that paper was what she had been given.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:165:It was the next evening, the hundred and fourth, that she came to the word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:195:*the Quieting*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:199:It was made out of a verb, like the other; it named a thing that went on happening, to whatever came over the line, for as long as it stayed there. It did not say how. It did not say why. It took *the* in front, which made it one thing, and it would take a place after it without complaint: the Quieting at the Fallow Ring. She could write *inside* before it, and *the edge of*, and *a site of*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:209:She woke before light on the hundred and fifth day, and it was there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:269:"The Quieting." She made a face. "It sounds like what you do to a baby."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:3:He slept through the third night, and woke on the hundred and second morning to find that a forge was only a forge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:21:The woman's eyebrows went up. She looked over Lira's head at the wicket in the northern fence, beyond which her goats were, and Cael could see her going along them one by one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:55:"It's the hundred and second day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:67:"Earned, a hundred and thirty-two. In hand, a hundred and eight, less six." The hinge creaked. "A hundred and two. It is the first that I have paid out. And been glad to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:125:"He's been like that since before it was light," said Brom. "I've not said eleven words. It was very restful."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:189:"Nineteen."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:193:"Nineteen," said Brom, and lifted his cup. Karis creaked it after him. Seln inclined his head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:211:*Hundred and second day past the Line. Nineteen. Written at Thornwater, in the steading's common room, the fire lit for us. Witnesses: four of my own, and a healer who's a stop.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:353:That was what brought the cold out on him. There was a leaf in the old volume of Hesk's book, the oldest unexplained thing that he owned, with a session's number at its head. Something had once come out of him in a practice yard that was none of his fragments, and it had come from under the breastbone, near the middle of him; a big hand had been laid on him afterward, and its owner had frowned and said that there was a quiet in there, under the noise. He had written the place down. He had turned that leaf over at every inventory since, and added nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:383:*Daeva's rematch, Reydan's answer — both ride east. Nineteen. Ten fragments. Hesk's package is on a ferry somewhere. The last one said mind the shape. I'm starting to.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:5:"I was fourteen. It was a district office on a cold morning, with a chalk circle on the boards for me to stand in. Something woke behind my breastbone, as it wakes in everybody on that day: a small light at the edge of my eye. You'd call it an Arbiter." He kept his voice low and his breath slow, as she had taught it to go. "It looked at me for eleven seconds, and I counted them. There's a plate let into the desk in those offices, which lights with the name of your Path. For eleven seconds it stayed dark."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:79:"That's the hundredth."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:145:"There's a line in there about what it's to cost you. Whatever I like, you've written, down to my walking north without you on the road. I've been paid in a good many ways out here. I've had a coat, and a cold fowl, and a shoe on the mule. I never yet had a patient hand me his own fine, ready counted, before I'd said what was owing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:155:It went into him more deeply than anything that she had yet said. He had priced a great many outcomes in a loft: her anger, and her contempt, and a road going north with nobody on it that he knew. He had not once thought that she might simply decline to tell him, and leave the question standing open in him for good, in the one place where no leaf of his could shut it and no price of his could buy it shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:189:It was still the hundredth morning, and the sun had not yet reached the well, when Oryn looked down and saw that she was holding a book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:3:The fourth finding was not about stone, and Karis gave it on the hundred and eighth morning in a different voice from the others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:13:"One. A bar of iron of the registry's pattern, bearing its stamp and the number thirty-one, stands on the edge of the Quieting at the Fallow Ring, a boot's width from the place where I found that edge with my feet. Two. By its rust, and by the turf grown against it, it has stood there about five years. Three. It was driven at the far end of a measure, so that whoever drove it had found the same circle that I paced, and had a cord or a chain to find it with. Four. I do not know what the number counts, or what was written down afterward, or where it is kept."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:29:He had been ruling a line through that iron since a wash on the road east. There had been a query sent down to a registry, asking which way certain people had gone; and a paper under a broker's seal, with a sum written twice; and a stake. At a bar of iron eleven days ago he had seen for the first time which way along that line he was looking. He had expected to find the whole of it on her leaf. It was not there. There was a stamp, and a number, and a length of time, and a measure; and under those she had written, plainly, the three things that she did not know.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:59:It was the evening of the hundred and ninth day, with the light gone from the shutter and the hold's lantern standing on the trestle among the chips. She had asked the four of them to stay down after the day's slips were counted; and when they had settled, on kegs and sacks and the foot of the ladder, she let a good while go by before she spoke. She had three leaves laid side by side on the scrubbed board, and a fourth beside them, which was the rubbing of the founding stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:125:The healer read the arm at first light on the hundred and tenth day, in the yard, with the frost still white on the well.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:131:He had done the sum on the ladder, and he did it again now, since he could not help it. If the eighth mark had turned, there would be a mending to pay for it, and then more days of watching on top of that. Every one of them would come off a round that was already sixteen behind, and be laid against a girl who had been told that she was not to carry them and would carry them regardless. If it had not turned, a healer went north in the morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:161:"You I can tell nothing. You'd know before I would." She rubbed her palms together. "I go in the morning. That's sixteen out of this gate. A round never gives a day back; it takes one. So I'll call it seventeen at Oxhollow, where there's a table that's been looking for me since the hundred and fourth."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:163:He had the sum before she had finished it: the eighty-fourth day at a ford, and twenty days of a round, and seventeen on top.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:173:Cael had not seen that it was a matter. He had known for eleven days that three boxes stood in that loft at a hundredweight each, that the carter who had brought them east was gone home with grain, and that the ferry was two days off. He had put all of it where he put the things that would be dealt with when there was leisure, behind a table and a birthday and a week of slips.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:241:"I know that it's left. It was left on the hundred and fourth."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:291:He gave it to her in order, and low, and he began by telling her that it had not started on a white floor. It had begun at noon on that day, a cord north of the grey stone, while five people were looking at what they had lost and got back; he had opened the read a pace short of the line, and walked, and it had run across the stride without a break. He had gone twenty strides in, to a boulder, and asked for a foot of Wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:331:"What was it like?" she said presently. It was her old voice, the one that she had kept for the rail of a practice floor. "When you let them go, in the lane. You'd held them a hundred days. Was it heavy? Did they come slow?"

