editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:69:She had not spoken to him since the yard. She had sat at a board with him, and had eaten a goat; it was not the same thing, and he did not make the mistake of taking it for one. A person may sit at a table with somebody whom she has not let off, as she may mend a man whom she does not like.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:251:"I have a name for it. I want it said to four people before it goes into the box, as he says a line under his eave. Then any of you may call it wrong."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:139:It was there. His boot came down on the frozen earth of the yard as it had come down all morning. But he felt it come down half a beat after it had done so, as a man hears an axe from across a valley after he has watched it fall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:241:On the platform by the western gate the bowman had turned round. It was the one hour of the day when nothing had ever come up that valley, and he seemed to keep it for looking inward, at the hold. He had watched a boy fall about a yard eleven times, and get it right, and he stood with his bow at the length of his arm and gave no sign that it was any business of his. Then his eyes went to the girl on the coping, who had seen a fault from thirty feet with one arm tied, and they stayed there a good deal longer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:291:He carried eleven sacks more before the light went, on feet that told him of each rung a little after he had climbed it, and he did not fall off the ladder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:99:She shut the door and let the peg fall. She did not take the stool. She carried the book to the sill, into the square of light, and laid it open on the strip of canvas among her own things, between the shears and the horn cup; and Cael thought that he had never seen an odder row.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:233:She had eaten, and had her two hours on her back, with the door shut and the book on the sill. She came out and washed to the elbow in the bucket, and she washed more slowly than she ever did, as a carter walks who thinks that his axle may be cracked and would sooner not hear it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:149:"That bone's a month old today. I said a month, on the mountain. It may run."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:185:"So that's two boxes on two backs, and one left over, and a case that the quiet one won't put down." She counted it on her fingers. "And a girl who's to fall on her face sooner than put a hand out, on a road that's ice in the ruts. Who steadies her, when the pair of you have a hundredweight apiece?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:251:"So I cannot tell you how it was made, since I have nothing to compare it to. But a thing may be dated without knowing how it was made, if it has stood in the weather, and that has." She took up the cup of wands. "I wrote down which way every stone faced. I did it from habit, and it is the best thing that I did on that floor."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:147:She took the place nearest the door. She sat on the end of the bench with her boots drawn under her, as a person sits who may have to get up, and laid her two hands on the board where she could see them. Cael watched her do it. She ate at seven gates, round after round, whatever was put before her, and went out again in the morning; and she sat at this one like a woman who has sat at a great many tables and stayed at none of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:161:"Then you may have my bread," said Seln, "all of it, with my compliments," and sat down.

