editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:135:She led the animal up until its forefeet were against the stone, and laid her whole hand on the warm neck under the mane, and stood with her head bent, reading whatever it is that a healer reads in a beast. Then, keeping her hand where it was, she stepped over, so that she stood inside with her arm stretched back across the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:137:She was a long time at that one. The mule turned its head and nosed her elbow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:139:The eighth was the longest, and she did nothing in it at all. She let go of the mule and walked three strides into quiet ground and stood there, square, with her arms hanging and her face toward the pale thing on the hill; and he counted a hundred before she moved. He thought that he understood it. She had told four holds' worth of carters, for thirty rounds, that she had no business in that place. She was finding out what she was when she stood in it: a woman in a brown coat, with a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:149:It was also the only thing that he had ever watched that woman do for herself. She had not done it for a table. She had got up before the light to do it where nobody would be looking, and she had found that somebody was, and had said nothing, and gone to see to a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:161:"You could. And if she puts a foot in a rut, you come off, and you come off onto that." Oryn had looked at the sling. "A fall off a mule's a fall. You walk."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:177:"You've got his other boot up there on the pack. If you say one word about my putting it on to match, I'll have it off that mule and you'll wear it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:187:It was not a decision. Lira had caught that small person off a ladder, and out of a chair, and once by the collar on the edge of a wash; her left hand knew the way. It was out of the sling to the wrist before any part of her had been consulted. She felt the wool go slack on her neck and the fingers open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:193:It was too slow, and it was too clumsy. A right arm is not where a left arm would have been. Karis struck it and slid, and went down on one knee in the rut with a jolt that brought a noise out of her throat, a croak, the first sound that she had made since the lane; and the notebook flew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:197:Karis looked up at her. She looked at the sling, and at the hand that had gone back into it, and at Lira's face. Then she got up by herself, brushing her knee, and found her pencil, and wrote something quite short and held it out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:201:The healer had not broken step. She was ten yards ahead, on the low side of the mule, and she had her head turned on her shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:211:The boy was on the mule's other side, at the off shoulder. He was breathing to her beat, as he had learned to on a road while she was not looking, and every so often he stood still for the space of a breath with his weight on the right leg. She had counted his strides between. It was eleven when they left the stone. By the spring, where they halted for the night, it was nearer forty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:213:He had looked across the mule at her when Karis fell. He had seen the hand come out, and seen it go back. He had not said anything, and he had not looked away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:215:*Later.* It was still on her side. She could wait. She had waited four days on a mountain to admit that a mule had a kind eye.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:219:They had a fire that night, at the spring, and Seln made the bread.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:241:Lira laughed, and put her right hand to the sling as though it had jarred her, and went on laughing. Karis had her notebook out. She wrote for some time, with her tongue between her teeth, and turned the leaf to the fire.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:249:"No," said Oryn, without looking up from her heel. "Tomorrow, if it wants to. Not because you've a joke ready."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:253:He sat among them with the cake warm in his hand. He had walked a day at a mule's shoulder with nine things shut, and they had gone back under his refusal like dogs that had been run; the heaviest of them lay easier tonight, and the cold had left his fingers at about noon. He had a sore leg and an arm that was coming back. He was, he supposed, mending.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:255:He looked round the fire at five faces. Every one of them had watched a thing go north out of a lane that they had been certain could not happen, and had walked beside him for a day without a word about it. They had not gone quiet, or careful. The big man had let another person make his bread sooner than leave the others without, and the small one had made a joke of it on paper, and Lira had told a carter's boot what she thought of it. It was the same crew that had sat at a fire above a wash, with a healer added, who was looking at her own heel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:261:He did not have one. He ate his bread, and it was very good, and he said so; and Brom told him that there was no call to be unkind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:267:Cael noticed it as a person notices that a noise has ceased. He had been walking at the mule's shoulder since first light, across the last of the pale grass, and he reached for the number of his strides and found that he had none; he had not stood still since the spring. He tried the calf on purpose, a long step and then a quick one. It was sore, and it was a leg.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:271:He looked across the mule's withers at the healer. She was on the low side, as she always was, with her hand on the cheek-strap and her eyes on the road, going at the one pace that she had. She had said the ninety-ninth for his ribs, at a gate, and the hundredth for the other thing. The hundredth was tomorrow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:273:He had known what the other thing was since a yard at Oxhollow. He had heard her describe it, in a healer's words, and had watched four faces change; and after that he had put it where he put the things that he would look at when there was leisure. There had been a letter to ration, and a line to pace, and a ring. For three days there had been an arm in a sling, and a hill that nobody spoke of. He had been glad of every one of them, and he saw now what he had been glad of them for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:275:It was a mile to the fork. He walked it at a mule's shoulder with a whole leg, and for the first time in fifteen days he let himself look.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:281:The second thing took him the rest of the mile. A woman was walking on the low side of a mule, six feet from him, who had added a stop to the only thing she owned in order to read a boy whom she could not read; and she had not the least idea that the boy might come away from her table with a piece of what she read with.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:283:They came down to the fork an hour after noon, and up the valley with the smoke of a hold standing over the fence in front of them. The east gate was open. He had gone out by it five days before with five other people and a calfskin, to pick up something that somebody had left lying.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:287:He had no business at that gate. He kept the western one, and in three stays Cael had seen him leave his platform once, to watch six people go out by the east; and he was standing beside the east gate now, with his bow at the length of his arm, like a man who has been coming to the same place at the same hour for five days. He looked at nobody but Lira. He watched her come up the last fifty yards on the flat of her foot, with a strip of grey wool across her and a hand tucked under her collarbone, and he did not lift two fingers from the stave. He took off his cap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:299:"I can see what it went for." She looked at the sling again. "Is that one of mine she's wearing?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:329:He put his boot up on the coping. She undid a knot that she had once called a good one, and dropped a strip of grey blanket on the ground, and turned the calf to the light. There was a seam down it, pale and dry, a hand long, lying over two older marks; she ran her thumb the length of it and did not shut her eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:339:She said it as she would have said a weight. He felt it go into him like a key. She had told him that at a ford: when he was not broken she would run the long one, and she wished to feel it not read him properly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:353:*You get told.* He had four people waiting in a loft, and he knew already what he was going to lay in front of them. Those three words would be in the middle of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:365:There were five people on it, under the window of the back room. They had seen a mule come up the valley and had come out and sat down, as eight had sat down at Oxhollow, and one of them was a man with his shin wrapped. She did not eat first. She went along them at her stride and a half, with two fingers for each wrist and her lips moving to ten, and at the end of the bench she turned round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:371:It was the same yard. He had not looked at it when he came in. There was the sweep of the well, and the cart-yard, and a boy forking straw into the barn under the loft where two boxes waited; the geese went by in a line, and somebody in the long house was scouring a pot. He had gone out of this place five days ago and stood on a floor that matched no stone in the world, and held a hound's head, and carried a light down a hill. Nothing here had altered by so much as a goose, and he found that he had been counting on that without knowing it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:377:She went up it sideways, in the end, with her right hand and both knees, and her left elbow tight against her ribs; and at the top she sat down on the loft's edge with her legs hanging, breathing hard, and looked at the sling as a person looks at a dog that has stayed when it was told.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:5:The spark took on the third stroke. Karis's small brass lantern had come out of the middle box, and when its flame stood up Cael saw the camp that six people had made without meaning to. They were ten yards west of the grey stone, on the old line of the road, with the mule standing over them and her ears laid back. A long grey thing lay between them and the stone, where it had come down out of the air, and nobody looked at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:7:Beyond the stone the slope went up into the dark. At the top of it there was still a faint paleness where the floor lay. Between that and the lamp, along sixty yards of grass, ran a strip of black no wider than his hand, where a wall had stood for a count of six; the frost was coming down on it already.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:9:Lira was on her back in the grass, and the healer knelt beside her. She had the cloth off the arm already, three strips of her own shirt and two pads of a linen cuff, and had laid it in a heap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:27:Brom had the bow between his knees already. He set his boot in the stirrup a third time, with four fingers that he had not yet let anybody see, and drew, and Cael heard his breath go in through his teeth. He laid a bolt on it, which left three in the sleeve. Then he walked ten yards to the north and stood there with his back to the lantern, a broad dark shape with a box still on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:31:There was one thing more to do before she went under. It was Karis's to do, and Karis could not; she sat in the grass with her two hands at her throat and her eyes on him. She held, as a one-armed man at a plank held, that a thing not said was not a finding; and so he said it for her, aloud, as Pike read a line before he wrote it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:41:"Mine's a calf, and an arm that's been struck. And what went up that hill with a light was me. I did it on purpose, to take them off the lane. I've no more to say about it tonight than that it was done."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:45:"Is that calf worse than this arm?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:61:There had been four fingers of it in the glass when Brom struck the flint; he had noted that, as he noted everything. It went down by the breadth of one of them while a square woman knelt in the grass with her eyes shut. He held the flame where she had told him to hold it, and kept it from moving.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:67:He could not read what was in them. He had been able to read that face across a yard since a lane in Ardenmere, and tonight he could not. It was not anger, which he knew, and it was not fear either. She looked at him steadily, as she might have looked at a card that had come up with a name on it which she had not expected.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:69:His left arm had gone dead to the elbow before the first quarter was out. He hung the lantern's ring over that wrist, like a bucket on a hook, and held the wrist up with the other hand. Something in his right arm had begun to ask about the lantern's brass rim. *Edge?* It had put that question to half the world for a season, patiently, and tonight for the first time it had been told yes; it asked now like a child that has been given one sweet. He told it no, and it asked again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:73:He listened for it the whole hour, and he learned what that hillside sounded like at night. The pale grass never stopped. The wind went over it from the north in long breaths, and it hissed and lay down and stood again, so that he was always hearing something come and finding that it was only the country. Twice Brom shifted his feet. Once the mule blew, and once, far off toward the north, a bird got up and cried and settled. There was a small dry ticking, close by, which he could not place until he saw that it was the frost, taking hold on the wet cloth in the heap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:79:It was closed. That was all that he let himself see: skin, where there had been none, and on it two short rows of small pink marks, one row on the upper side and one beneath, each mark no bigger than a barleycorn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:91:The healer had not finished. She kept hold of the wrist, and she bent over Lira until her face was a hand's breadth away, and she spoke as she had spoken to Brom about a cuff, slowly, so that there should be no second meaning in any piece of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:117:"No. You were about to not ask, and I'd sooner say it than watch you." Oryn's voice had gone to a thread. "I'll tape it in the morning. I've nothing left to see it with."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:123:There were six of Thornwater's blankets on that mule, and there were six people.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:129:He cut the bread as well, a round loaf from the hold's oven, into six, with the same knife that had cut the sling; and he left the sixth piece on a pannier lid by Oryn's head, where she would find it before she found anything else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:141:He read them, and nodded. She took the leaf back and held the pencil over it, and he watched the point come down to the paper and lift, and come down. He knew what was in that small head. She had been ten strides in front of him with a hound's jaw across her fists, and she had seen a thing arrive that had not come; and after it a crack, and a light, and something that rang. She had nine questions at the least, and she had been waiting all her life for a source like this one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:151:She bound the calf as she would have ruled a table. He supposed that she had watched a healer do it under a rock, and had kept it, as she kept everything; she was not gentle, and she was very exact, and when she tied it off the knot lay flat. She patted the leg once, absently, as she had patted a box on Brom's back. Then she went and lay down on her side against that box with her back to all of them, in Seln's coat, as she had lain on a mountain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:167:Across the pads of four fingers there was a cut, straight, as though it had been ruled. They had bled down into his cuff and dried. He had drawn that string three times with them, the last time when they were already open, and he had stood an hour in the frost with the stock in that hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:177:That was the camp. Nobody had said a word to Cael about a hill. They had eaten bread beside him, and passed him the water, and Seln had put the last blanket round his shoulders without a remark, as he would have put it round anybody. Four of them knew what he was and had not known until tonight that it reached in there. One of them knew nothing, and had asked him whether his calf was worse than an arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:185:Karis had looked at the lantern's glass before she slept, and held up three fingers, which was what an hour had left in it. He set it on the lid of the box and opened Hesk's book to the page that had stopped short. The entry there was in his own hand and a few hours old; it said that the dogs were inside, and that he had told nobody, and he read it as he might have read the writing of somebody he had known as a boy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:193:*Lira: left forearm bitten through and held; mended at the line, an hour, by lantern. One arm till first snow, by order. Right ankle gone a third time, and not mended. Karis: throat; no voice. Brom: four fingers, on a string. Seln: nothing. Oryn: spent to the bottom, and asleep on the road.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:195:*Mine: left calf open on the old seam. Left forearm struck; dead to the elbow. Ribs taped, three days to run; they held.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:199:*The read: far lip to the rut. One head turned for it. I had my eyes in the ground when Lira went up on the rim.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:201:*Shadow: once, ten strides, in the lane. Four saw it who have always known. The fifth had a man and a box between. I did that sum before, not after. It holds. Cold hands.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:213:*Storm: three lanes, in the rut. The read goes to the air for those. That's the calf.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:221:*Twelve. With the ford, thirteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:223:*Deployment since the Line: thirteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:235:He read it over. It was the kind of sentence that Karis struck out of other people's notes, since it had a likeness in it. He let it stand, since he had nothing truer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:253:The mule shifted. Somewhere to the north Brom cleared his throat, and was quiet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:255:He waited for the next thing, and found that he was holding his breath as he had held it under a healer's hand. He had a great many sentences ready for her, and not one of them was an answer. He did not know why. He had not known at noon, and he had kept that from her at noon with a pencil going; and she, of all the people alive, had the right to ask him how long.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:7:They went down in the order that Cael had given. Seln was in front with the narrow knife, and Karis behind him with her hand on her coat. Then came the healer and Lira, as close as two people in one sack: Oryn on the right with her shoulder in Lira's armpit, and Lira's bound arm held up against her own collarbone, and the great boot coming down whole at every step like a duck's foot. Brom walked after them with the bow across his chest. Cael came last, four strides behind Brom's box, with Lira's knife in his hand and the read under his soles. He had the only eyes in the file that were not wanted for carrying somebody, and a rear guard needs room to see what it is guarding.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:81:He could see the stone. It was a paler thing at the lane's end, the size of a loaf. The mule stood beyond it with her long ears forward, hobbled, and in no fear at all; nothing in that grass wanted anything that she had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:97:Seln was five strides beyond her, turning, with the knife coming up; and between Seln and Karis one of the three that Cael had never told apart had stepped out into the lane and stood there, broadside, with its head level. Lira and the healer were five strides on the near side; Oryn had stopped dead, with her whole weight under Lira, and both of them were looking at the thing on the ground. Brom was a stride behind the two women, with the bow at his shoulder already.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:109:There was a third thing, and it was already lifting. It had lifted at sunset behind the other eight, as it lifted at every dusk, with the one offer that it ever made. It did not put anything into the air. It was made to take something out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:125:It was ten strides. On the tenth he let it go, with his hands already on the animal's back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:135:He was not there. He did that with his own two feet and nothing in them. A girl on a painted floor had taught it to him before she taught him anything else, and had told him that nothing is thrown which has not first been let go by. He stepped off its line by the width of his body. He took it as it passed, by one foreleg and a handful of hide, and added what he had to what it was already doing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:207:A Storm lane wanted the air, and to have the air he must give up the ground; at that weight the read would carry one or the other, never both. He let the turf go. It was like shutting an eye. The pace and a half of the world that he had walked in since the far lip went out from under his soles. He was a boy running up a ditch in the dark, with a flame in front of his face and nothing to tell him where his feet would land.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:219:It was in the second that his left boot came down on a stone. It was a round thing the size of a loaf, bedded in the rut's floor; he would have had it a stride and a half off with the read, and he had not got the read. The foot turned on it, and the calf took the whole of his weight slantwise, along a seam that a hound had made at a ford and a plate of shale had opened on a mountain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:259:It took it late. For the first beat there was nothing there at all, and the animal's whole weight went into his legs as a cart's tail goes into a man in a lane. His knees went. He pitched forward down the hill with its chest on his shoulders, and kept the knife up, and put out the other arm. His left forearm met the ground under the two of them. Then the thing that a fighter had once put through him, and left with him, arrived and did what it was for. There was only one road open to it. The whole of that blow went down one bone into the hill, and he felt it ring from the elbow to the wrist like a struck rail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:267:It was two hundred yards to the stone, downhill. He ran it on a calf that was open, and a side that had been told *there* too many times to go on saying it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:269:He had no read. He had a flame at the end of his arm, which showed him ten feet of grass and blinded him to everything beyond. He could not see the stone. He knew where it was as he knew where his own hand was, and he ran down the dark toward the place where a lane ended.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:283:She had laid them along the line's inside edge. That was what her feet had been finding since first light: the last hand's breadth of ground where a point would hold, so close to the place where an Arbiter stopped that an animal coming out would meet it with its next stride.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:287:There was grass under him that was only grass, and a mule's shape, and a big man down on one knee a yard in front of him with a bow-stock at his cheek. Brom's eye was not on him. It was on something past his hip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:301:It went up out of the grass along sixty yards, thirty to the north of the stone and thirty to the south, a wall of yellow-white fire as high as a man's knee and as straight as her wrist could draw a curve that size. He felt her discharge go past him like the door of a furnace. It was the first thing that anybody but himself had declared on that hillside since noon, and after what he had carried down the slope it seemed to him a clean and ordinary thing, like bread.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:313:Two were dead: one on a white floor, with a bolt in its eye, and one a yard from his boots. Seven had gone, north and south, and nobody on that hill could have said more of them than that. Six people were on the western side of a grey stone. Four of the six were hurt, and he would have the count of that by lamplight. He knew already that every hurt but his own had been taken by somebody who had no means of answering. Nine things lay under his hand again, and they had not yet stopped shaking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:315:There were no instruments, and there would be no hundred marks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:9:They stood on the east, at the mouth of the den, and everything that they wanted was on the west. The floor lay between, twenty-six strides across. From its western lip the lane that the cord had trodden ran down through the grass, as straight as a rule, for two hundred and seven yards; and at the end of the lane there was a grey stone and a mule, and beyond the stone a Path was a Path again and a healer's hands were hands. That made two hundred and thirty-three yards from where his boots were.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:27:They did not hurry, and it was that which he liked least. They spread out among the eleven stones as a crew spreads out in its own yard at the end of a day. One sat and scratched. The pale one stood at the middle of the round, by the place where a cord had ended, with its nose down on the grey.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:29:They had gone out by the east at first light, as Seln had read off bare earth, and they had come home by the west, up the one track on the whole of that hillside which six people had made since noon. He thought of a healer at the far end of that track, counting to ten on a wrist, twice, with her Path running; he did not know that it bore, and he put it by for whoever kept findings.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:33:It was Seln, and he said it as he would have read out a line from a bill of lading.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:43:Behind him the slope fell away to the east, and the line on that side was nearer by twenty-six yards. It was also the hounds' own door. There were three runs across it and not a stride of it that any of the six had walked; the grass stood hip-high the whole way down, the ground was steep, and in a quarter of an hour it would be dark. If they came out alive on that side, they would be half a circle of open country from a mule. The west was a lane trodden flat by their own feet, with a stone at the end of it that five of them could find with their eyes shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:45:"There's a bow on that mule," said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:75:Cael opened the read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:97:It stood on the rolled edge a yard from Brom's right hip, with its head no higher than his belt, and it looked him over as a man looks over a cart that he may buy. Cael had it inside the read from the instant its forefeet touched the lip, and he felt the weight of it come forward, slowly, off the haunches and onto the forelegs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:125:"I dropped that first one hard enough to stun a calf. And this one walked up to the same place as if I'd never been born. It watched me do it. It was standing there on the floor."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:173:She ran along the rim itself. It was a thing no wider than a roof-ridge, round on top, with the white a finger below it on one side and the turf a hand below on the other, and she went along it as he had watched her go along a ledge under a mountain, close and quick, with her arms a little out. The great boot came down every second stride with a slap. There was no burst in it and no Path, since there could be none; it was a girl of thirteen on the lanes at Fenmark, who had only legs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:189:The pale one took her by the left forearm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:217:It was the size of a calf. He bore the long head down until the jaw lay on the floor, with Lira's forearm lying on the floor between the teeth, and he put the whole of his weight on it and kept it there. He had the thing inside the read, every pound of it; and each time that it gathered itself to wrench he had the gathering a breath before the wrench came, in the neck and the shoulders, and leaned his own weight to the place where it meant to go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:229:Everything that he carried had come up, as he had not felt it come since a mountain. Compression showed him the skull under his palms and how little it would take. Wind wanted his feet, and hers. And Ember had the place already, as it had had it once in a ford: he was looking into a yellow eye from a foot away, and it would set a point on anything that he looked at for long. *There.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:231:He could have ended it in a breath, three ways, and he went through what came after as quickly as he would have gone through a bout. Whichever he chose would go out of him on that floor a great deal louder than a healer's count, and eight heads would come round. There were two hounds within a yard of Brom's legs already, and Karis and the healer stood at the lip with nothing in their hands. He could take one animal, perhaps two; he could not take eight off four people who were standing inside the length of a cart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:263:"Thirteen feet," said Karis. Her voice was a thread. She could no more have helped it than he could.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:271:Cael did not move his hands. He had made the thing as still as he knew how to make it, and that was the whole of what he had to give. Nine fragments lay under his refusal, and not one of them could do what was wanted, which was to let a friend try. He looked across thirteen feet at one grey eye over a bow-stock, narrowed, and at a face that would have done anything on earth sooner than loose that string.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:299:Her voice was quite level. She was a different person already, and one that he had not met. She was a woman on her knees in the grass with nothing in her hands, and her linen was two hundred yards off on a mule, with everything else that she had left there at noon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:307:He held them. The teeth had met through the arm. He knew what that was, from the inside of his own calf, and he knew that his calf had been the smaller matter. He had seen her hurt in a dozen ways since a lane in Ardenmere, and he had never before seen so much of her on the outside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:309:The healer had dragged the tail of her own shirt out from under the brown coat, and she was tearing it upward, with one hand and her teeth, into strips three fingers wide. She did not wind them herself. She had lifted Lira's forearm across her own knee, to keep it higher than the heart, and she would not let go of it for anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:327:The lane went down in front of him into the dusk, a thin pale stroke with the grass standing dark on either hand, and he could not see the stone at the end of it, or the mule.

