editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:110:| The edge territories: holds, fords, one town, no Arbiter stations, no paper | By report and the B6 keeper's account | Lowmarch and the board learned on day 9 |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:125:- **Seln's locked case:** the cache. He brought it east in his own post satchel; since the shelf it rides under the cart's canvas with the three boxes (edition B6 ch60). Once the cart is sold in Lowmarch's first week (#23) it goes back on its strap and is on him on every road. **Never opened in this book.** [B6-reconciled 2026-10-11: coordinator ruling]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:126:- **The cart and the horse** (edition B6 ch56, ch60). **C1 default:** sold in Lowmarch's first week. The sale and the rest of the B6 purse become a sealed reserve the crew does not live on. The log's "in hand" counts only the board purse. OWNER #23 governs: sell cart and gelding in Lowmarch's first week; put the sale and remaining Book 6 purse into a sealed reserve the crew does not live on. [B6-reconciled 2026-10-11: audit D3, N7]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:152:| 101 | 8 (entered) | 11th, said on day 112 | ten up |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:182:- Cael: age; fragments (count, any change, deployment since the Line); injuries open; idle-state notes; the still place's status; money (earned / in hand).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:196:- **#23 executed on the page:** the gelding went to the ferryman and the cart to the smith in Lowmarch's first week; the sale and the rest of the Book 6 purse are sewn and waxed into a reserve under five thumbprints at the bottom of the third box. "In hand" never counts the reserve.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:198:- **The road rule is a road custom** (walk quiet, steel, no fire, don't declare). Fires, voices and native Paths are open in Lowmarch's meadow and in a steading's yard; travel roads and exposed fence-lines call for the hold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:210:- **Lowmarch's ground (flag):** the town on the river's east bank; a cable ferry, a square scow wound across by a drum in a round thatched capstan-shed, with a beast walking the circle; a copper a wheel and a copper a beast; the lower inn with a deep eave, above the landing; the upper inn on the rise, with two chimneys; the smith between; the meadow below, half a mile, with one great ash; standing-money a copper a night, hay by the net; the grass behind the landing. Roads out: the west road, the north water road, the east road over a saddle (a cart's width to the saddle, a mule's gauge beyond), the south road (it forks), and the cold road, which leaves the north road at a dead tree.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:212:- **How a line is made (flag):** the price is left with Pike at posting; a crew takes a slip at the plank and Pike marks it; the job's witness marks the slip; Pike reads the line aloud before he writes it and again after, then pays. Pike's sign for the five: five short strokes. Marks seen: the ferryman's (a ring with a dot), the north-water steading's (three uprights and a bar), the hide-woman's (a square with four pegs), Thornwater's (a thorn twig over a wavy line), the landing broker's (a hook and a bar, in wax and burned on his goods).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:220:- **Roles (all unnamed):** the courier (about fifty, a split lip long healed, a hat cut away on the right, a hatchet in a saddle loop); the three carts' people, who are one group throughout: the old carter with two mules, who takes the shade of the ash; the boy of about fifteen; the hide-woman, who is also the wash's hirer; the ferryman and his boy; the standing-money boy; the smith; the landing broker and his clerk; the old man of the north-water steading, his white dog, his goat; the Thornwater carter (short, thick, twenty-two years on his road); the steading's woman (broad, a grey braid); the bowman; the north-road woman captain (a red cloth round her hair; two men and a girl); the eleven-bale captain (a new coat).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:226:- **Thornwater (flag):** a grain hold: river-country grain stored and fetched by carts from holds farther east. A fence of split posts as high as a man's head, a quarter of a mile about; a west gate with a bar and a platform beside it; a wicket north to grazing and a stone hut; an east gate and a road on up the valley (walked by Seln for two hours, at noon; nothing said of it); a long house, a barn with the loft over it, a cart-yard, a well with a sweep, geese.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:227:- **The carter's map (flag):** a scraped calfskin in oak-gall ink and burnt stick: roads as double lines, fords as three wavy strokes, holds as a square with a dot, Lowmarch as a ring with a bar, days as ticks, and bare hide wherever a wheel has no business. Given to Karis for the wheel Brom rebuilt. Five roads run off its edges.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:228:- **Teague, as seen (flag):** forty or so, middle height, heavy in the forearm, cropped hair grey over the ears; a black leather band laced on the right wrist; a hand-axe in his belt; a coat mended by somebody else. His week on the cold road is salt on mules to the top holds before frost shuts the pass, along two miles of ledge; he has done it nine times. He thinks only "the spear", "the bow" (a bad leg), "the boy". One other crew on the sheet could clear the ford and is up the north road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:234:**Calendar.** The evening of the **forty-ninth day past the Line**, in the lower inn at Lowmarch. No season, month or English weekday name is on the page; "Fifth-day" appears twice (ch4, ch5) as the academy's own word. Days on the page: 2 (night), 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9, 10–12, 14, 15–21 (drills; the catalogue on the 18th), 22–24, 30 (Seln, the cold road), 33, 41, 42, 43, 44–46, 48, 49. Weather: warm, dusty, long light; the roads "dry right through" from about day 46; one storm over the eastern ridge on day 33. The next movement opens on day 51.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:240:**Lira.** Wind; Iron, Rank One, the certificate in her coat. Uninjured (palms skinned at the wash on day 33, healed by day 41). One burst went off by itself on the gravel bar in the first week of drills; none since. She can stop dead from a full run. She wears a long knife bought at Lowmarch's smith. She has been noticed, once, by Thornwater's bowman.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:246:**Seln.** Shadow; Bronze, said once at a fire. Uninjured. Has not lifted his Path since the crossroads fire. Has walked the first hours of eight roads, five out of Lowmarch and three out of Thornwater; on day 49 he says, in one sentence, that he has walked the first part of all five out of Lowmarch. Has the prints and the forty-five-yard line by the white stone in his head, a hundred and ten yards from the gate (a hundred and four of his own paces), and has told nobody. **The case is on its strap, on him, shut.**
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:259:- **Road culture is contextual:** ordinary meadow/yard use is open, while travel roads and exposed fence-lines trigger the hold. Do not retroactively make Lowmarch's meadow silent or Pathless (`manuscript/chapter-02.md:55-63`; `manuscript/chapter-04.md:165-183`).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:262:- **Lowmarch's working geography and institutions persist:** cable ferry/capstan, lower inn/eave/plank, upper inn, smith, meadow/ash, road fan, public read-before/read-after procedure, iron payment box, sailcloth wall and Pike's double look (`manuscript/chapter-02.md:13-87,145-163`; `manuscript/chapter-03.md:251-277`; `manuscript/chapter-07.md:29-63,181-219`).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:263:- **Asset state after #23:** no cart or horse; the reserve is sealed under masonry chips in the third box and opens only by all five; the boxes lodge over the ferry drum when the crew is near Lowmarch; Seln's closed case is back on its strap (`manuscript/chapter-03.md:107,123-185`; `manuscript/chapter-04.md:277`; `manuscript/chapter-05.md:55-65`).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:305:- **Lowmarch:** undirected eastbound mail stops at Pike's plank; he gives out each piece twice and reads letters at dusk to people who have no letters; a spike under the plank holds finished slips; he rewrites the whole wall on fresh sized sailcloth when the order changes; a crew entered as *the shingle pair* is thirteenth from day 60.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:306:- **Teague:** back down the cold road on the 57th; read the struck slip twice and the five's line; took two holds' slips from the north end of the plank and went up the north water at next light. Not seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:313:**Calendar.** The evening of the **sixty-ninth day past the Line**, under the lower inn's eave at Lowmarch. No season, month or English weekday name is on the page ("the thaw" in three places; "frost" once). Days on the page: 51, 52, 53, 54, 55–57 (the stick on the 56th; the carter back on the 57th), 58, 59, 60, 61, 62, 63, 64, 65, 66, 68, 69. Told, not shown: the dusk of the 49th at the ford; the 50th. Weather: dry roads; mist on the river at first light; cold coming up out of the wash after dark. The next movement opens on the morning of day 70.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:315:**Where everyone is.** All five at Lowmarch, sleeping in the meadow, with the three boxes in the loft over the ferryman's drum. Teague and his three are out on two holds' slips up the north water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:329:**Pike's book and the wall.** **Six lines** under the five names: the protected ford line (said on day 60) and *The well at the south fork. Air cleared, shaft drawn, six stones set. Four marks.* (day 64). **Twelfth of thirteen; Teague eleven up.** The ford slip is with the finished slips, struck with one line through Teague's name and five strokes beneath, and Thornwater's mark at the foot. Fourteen slips on the plank on day 60; a bare tack set by the left-hand post on the evening of day 69 for a hold's slip expected from the north-west.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:370:- **Why the job exists (flag):** an Oxhollow cart fetching Thornwater grain goes round by Lowmarch's ferry, eight days each way; the salt crews speak of a shelf over the high ground between, and a mule string once came over it in three days. Oxhollow is "a ford with a fence round it"; its keeper takes a copper a wheel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:371:- **Which way it is walked (flag):** east to west. The eastern foot is at the lower of the two crossings on the Thornwater road, where a side water comes in from the north; an old cart-track, grown over, climbs it for two hours to a gorge. Thornwater is not visited and the stretch is not walked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:378:- **The mountain (flag):** the fan (two hundred and sixty yards, a gully along its gorge-ward edge, rock islands; climbed in six stages); the bench (thirty feet at best, pitched outward a hand or two to the yard; slab, with tongues of loose plates where gullies cross); a stone dropped from the first hundred is heard at a count of four and a piece, and again off the far wall; four sleeping places under rock, each found by Seln; eight narrowings (eleven feet; nine; nine and a half; seven; eight; six and a hand; five feet nine with a crust at the rim; the eighth nine feet wide and broken clean across by a slot of two feet); the bay (two hundred yards by forty, floored with shale, a seep at its eastern corner); the pale rock, or fin (see the event list's amendment); the ledge (three hundred yards, six to eight feet wide, roofed in parts); the lip (thirty-one paces, four feet wide, tilted, forty-four scratched stones); the shoulder; a mule track down the western side; a valley road north-west to Oxhollow, a day and a half.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:388:- **Oxhollow (flag):** on the north bank of a broad shallow river, in a hollow, behind a palisade of split logs with a gate on the south side toward the ford and another on the north; a ford of a hundred yards of gravel under a foot of water; a yoke of red oxen with a chain, for drawing carts through; the keeper's low stone house with a turf roof between the gate and the water; a long house; a well; the healer's table of scrubbed ash on two trestles under a lean-to against the gate-house, with a bench along the wall. The keeper is old, bent, with a notched hazel tally-stick, and cannot read; he keeps his coin under a hearthstone. He would have sent three carts over the top at the next dry spell with a Force walking behind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:397:**Where everyone is.** The five are at Oxhollow, in the yard and at the ford, with three boxes on the ground against the gate-house wall. Oryn has gone north out of the gate at dusk with her mule, and is due at Thornwater on the 93rd. Teague and his three have crossed the ford southward at dusk with two mules, a day ahead of the five on the Lowmarch road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:422:Movement 4 already carries the dusk-of-84 location, 116 in hand / six owed, taped ribs, bound calf, new leg, missing right boot, Oryn north, Teague south, nine days to Thornwater, and the three boxes (`packets/MOVEMENT-004.md:18-20`). It must additionally inherit:
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:424:- The protected shale-back finding remains **derived, not tested** after an uncontrolled two-cause event; twenty-five are seen on day 81, while the missing five remain “heard, not sworn” (`manuscript/chapter-17.md:235-251`; `manuscript/chapter-19.md:195-203`; `manuscript/chapter-21.md:111-113,341-343`; fix 5).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:433:- Pike's board has not yet heard the traverse: six lines, twelfth of thirteen, Teague eleven up remain the last spoken standings until the marked slip reaches Lowmarch (`manuscript/chapter-14.md:439-465`; `AUTHOR-REPORT.md:174-176`). Hesk's letter is still unanswered, Lira's earlier question remains unanswered, and two stillhounds remain open downstream (`manuscript/chapter-14.md:439-451`; `STATE_LEDGER.md:329-331`).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:442:- **Cael's two private tests** (day 96, a quarter of an hour, a cord north of the stone, unwitnessed) are NOT deployments and are not logged as such. Deployment since the Line stays ONE. Shadow untouched. He has told nobody; his reasons are on the page and he distrusts them. The day-96 log omits the deployment line for the first time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:456:- **Oxhollow's grain cart (flag).** Since his carts "go round", the keeper sends one to Thornwater for grain on the 85th, eight days out and eight home. Its carter is his sister's son: a long, sleepy-faced young man with very large feet and two grey mules, who says about forty words in six days. The three boxes ride his bed from Oxhollow to Thornwater (85th–93rd) for nothing, because the five walk his road in front of him. No paper, no line. He rests the 91st at Lowmarch and loads grain at Thornwater on the 94th.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:458:- **The road Oxhollow–Lowmarch (flag):** south down a broad valley of alder and rough pasture, small fords, halting-grounds; on the fourth day two hundred yards of moss crossed by a causeway of laid logs (the carter's father's), about one log in six rotten at the heart. It reaches Lowmarch's river on the west bank and crosses by the ferry.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:468:- **The registry's printed sheet (flag):** east of the Line it shows a river or two without their bends, hills drawn alike, and seven dashed roads with no names that run out into white and stop; the third from the top crosses the first water (no ferry, no Lowmarch) and ends three fingers east of it, at about Thornwater's fence or an hour beyond. **"Void"** (flag): Seln says that in a registry's usage an entry is void when nothing is written against it, and supposes that a carter was once shown such a sheet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:469:- **Three blank circles on the carter's map (flag; see owner flag 4):** the bend east of Thornwater, and two round blanks that Karis had read as thrift, one in his hill-hatching a day south of Lowmarch and a little aside, one far to the south-east among his water-strokes with no road within a day. She rings all three in pencil and writes *guess* beside two. The hide is "a map of days" and will not take a straightedge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:474:- **The Ring's ground (flag; one picture for chapters 27–30):** at the crest of a long rise the ruts swing out to the north and come back to their line on the far side, keeping about forty yards from the line all the way round. The line crosses the old straight line of the road **eighty of Cael's strides** beyond the turn; Karis's grey stone marks it (the circle's western point). The camp is at the turn. The floor is three hundred yards from the turn. The perimeter is walked from the stone northward, with the ring on the right hand; Brom cuts a turf at each cord; the constant offset is **six feet and a half** to the cord.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:476:- **Hound sign outside:** three runs through the grass within twenty yards of each other on the eastern side, crossing the line both ways; none on the north side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:487:**Calendar.** Sunset on the **ninety-sixth day past the Line**, at the eastern lip of the Fallow Ring's floor. No season, month or English weekday name is on the page ("the cold term"; "frost"; "before the snow"; "fortnight"). Days on the page: 84 (dusk), 85, 86, 87, 88, 89, 90, 91, 92, 93, 94, 95, 96; and, in Teague's window, the evening of the 89th. Weather: frost every morning; dry roads; a north wind on the Fallow. The next movement opens where this one stops.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:503:**Teague.** At Lowmarch on the 91st with his three. He read the Fallow posting twice on the 89th and put it back, and took a fence that evening (the upper inn's paddock, in sight of the ferry; three days, five marks); he has no reason against the posting and has given none. He watched the five take it. The two holds' slips are his if the five are not down inside the fortnight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:539:- **The Ring:** nine hounds counted on the white; two dead by Brom's bolts (thirteen feet; six feet); seven away "north and south along the line's outside", direction unobserved. The crossbow was on the mule; Seln fetched it during the retreat round the floor and Brom spanned it by hand. Bolts: six, five in the sleeve from the 97th. Geometry as closed in Movement 4.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:551:- **The rain-rut (flag):** natural, on the swell's north-west flank; it begins under the floor's north-western lip and runs down a little north of west, knee-deep and a stride wide; abreast of the lane's sixty-yard mark it lies about forty yards north. Nothing made is added to the site.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:566:- **Oryn's route:** "the top gate of my round's eleven days north of here", by the road, at the mule's pace. No lateness figure is given.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:573:**Calendar.** The morning of the **hundredth day past the Line**, in Thornwater's back room, in the twenty-first minute of the long reading. No season, month or English weekday name is on the page ("first snow", "the snow", "the harvest", "frost"). Days on the page: 96 (sunset to night), 97, 98, 99, 100. Weather: frost every night; a north wind on the Fallow; ice on Thornwater's trough.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:591:**Hounds.** Nine came; two are dead (one on the floor, one drawn off a hundred yards south of the stone, outside the line); **seven went north and south along the line's outside** on the night of the 96th and were not seen, tracked or heard again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:597:**Open threads.** The telling, on the table, owed that minute. What it cost her, if anything. A leaf in a loft to be fetched and read. *Later*, on Lira's side. Seven hounds. A report going west with six on it. An arm slung until the snow, and an ankle that nobody has mended. Eleven sheets not yet looked at. Two rings marked *guess*. The top gate of a round, eleven days north.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:600:Movement 6 already carries the telling, birthday, Quieting name, tables, shooting lessons, mule decision, cash after the birthday, string-ban expiry and Oryn's northward window (`MOVEMENT-006.md:20-53`). Its author must additionally inherit these surviving-prose facts:
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:605:- **Oryn:** only Cael saw her eight dawn crossings; five Thornwater patients are waiting until after noon; she has not yet shown what the acquisition cost her; and her route's top gate is eleven travel-days north (`manuscript/chapter-35.md:119-151,339-367`; `manuscript/chapter-34.md:191-197`; `manuscript/chapter-37.md:206-244`).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:607:- **Accounts/open threads:** start day 100 with 108 in hand; the Fallow hundred remains unpaid in Pike's box; one sixty-yard four-strand rope, two HOLD slips and three replies remain owed; the completion report travels west saying six; seven hounds remain untracked north/south along the outside (`manuscript/chapter-24.md:19,247-251`; `manuscript/chapter-33.md:193-195`; `manuscript/chapter-34.md:273-307`; `STATE_LEDGER.md:499-505`).

